WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:22.350
Tim Ferriss: Hello, boys and girls, ladies
and germs.

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:23.800
This is Tim Ferriss.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.890
Welcome to another episode of The Tim Ferriss
Show.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:30.760
My guest today is a fan favorite, one of my
favorites, Jack Kornfield.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:37.260
So, Jack is one of the few people I have on
speed dial if I hit particularly acute existential

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:44.220
distress or anxiety, and this is roughly a
two-year anniversary since we recorded an

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:47.510
episode, which I suggest everyone listen to.

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:49.850
Right at the early stages of COVID.

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.010
This was in March, of 2020.

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:53.800
So who is Jack?

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.770
Jack Kornfield trained as a Buddhist monk
in the monasteries of Thailand, India, and

00:00:57.770 --> 00:00:58.770
Burma.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:02.940
He has taught meditation internationally since
1974 and is one of the key teachers to introduce

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.460
Buddhist mindfulness practice to the West.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:10.170
Jack co-founded the Insight Meditation Society
in Barre, Massachusetts with fellow meditation

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.610
teachers Sharon Salzburg and Joseph Goldstein.

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:13.610
Goldstein?

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:14.610
Jack Kornfield: Goldstein.

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:15.610
Tim Ferriss: Goldstein.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:16.610
It's the German.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:18.850
It's the spending time in Germany that always
messes me up here.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:22.280
And the Spirit Rock Center in Woodacre, California,
which is stunning.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:23.460
I have spent some time there.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.560
We talked about that in our first conversation.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.670
Over the years, Jack has taught worldwide,
led international Buddhist teacher meetings

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:32.090
and worked with many of the great teachers
of our time.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:34.450
He holds a PhD in clinical psychology.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:38.840
And I just want to mention an aside here,
one of the things that I love about you, Jack,

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:45.130
and respect about you is that you spend time
both in the deeply philosophical — I shouldn't

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:46.130
say both.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:52.200
There are many stems to this, the deeply contemplative,
but also in the deeply clinical.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:55.579
And you've worked with many, many different
patient groups.

00:01:55.579 --> 00:02:01.260
So I think that provides you with a very interesting
and eclectic broad spectrum toolkit.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:06.010
So I just want to say that for people who
may not be familiar with you.

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:08.149
And jumping back in.

00:02:08.149 --> 00:02:10.670
You are also a grandfather, husband, and activist.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:15.969
Some of your current projects include MMTCP,
a worldwide mindfulness teacher training,

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:17.590
Cloud Sangha, if I'm pronouncing that correctly.

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:18.739
Jack Kornfield: Yeah, got it.

00:02:18.739 --> 00:02:21.780
Tim Ferriss: This is another word that I have
trouble with.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:22.780
All right.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:23.840
Nailed it.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:29.349
S-A-N-G-H-A, a site offering access to expert
mindfulness teachers online in a positive

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:31.439
impact Wisdom Ventures fund.

00:02:31.439 --> 00:02:36.379
His books have been translated into 22 languages
and have sold more than 1.5 million copies.

00:02:36.379 --> 00:02:37.859
I have read many of them.

00:02:37.859 --> 00:02:42.709
They include The Wise Heart, subtitle, A Guide
to the Universal Teachings of Buddhist Psychology;

00:02:42.709 --> 00:02:48.910
A Path with Heart; After the Ecstasy, the
Laundry; one of my favorite titles, Teachings

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:54.639
of the Buddha; The Art of Forgiveness, Lovingkindness,
and Peace; Bringing Home the Dharma subtitle

00:02:54.639 --> 00:02:56.849
Awakening Right Where You Are.

00:02:56.849 --> 00:03:01.260
And his most recent book is No Time Like the
Present, subtitle, Finding Freedom, Love,

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:02.859
and Joy Right Where You Are.

00:03:02.859 --> 00:03:06.049
You can find him online, jackkornfield.com.

00:03:06.049 --> 00:03:12.939
That's K-O-R-N-F-I-E-L-D.com, on Twitter @jackkornfield,
Instagram, @Jack_Kornfield.

00:03:12.939 --> 00:03:15.219
Jack, it's so nice to see you again.

00:03:15.219 --> 00:03:16.260
Thank you for making the time.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.079
Jack Kornfield: It's a pleasure, Tim.

00:03:18.079 --> 00:03:23.089
Happy to see you and very glad to know that
you're in [BLEEP] and that you brought rain with

00:03:23.089 --> 00:03:24.089
you.

00:03:24.089 --> 00:03:28.579
You turned out to the rainmaker after all,
and we really need it out west.

00:03:28.579 --> 00:03:29.699
So way to go.

00:03:29.699 --> 00:03:30.700
Tim Ferriss: I thank you.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:31.700
Thank you.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:33.650
I do what I can where I can.

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:41.029
So I just need to figure out exactly how I
managed to precipitate this rain and replicate

00:03:41.029 --> 00:03:42.029
that.

00:03:42.029 --> 00:03:43.639
But that's a project right after the podcast.

00:03:43.639 --> 00:03:46.290
Jack, there's so many different places we
could start.

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:52.579
I thought I would begin with explaining one
of our most recent conversations and that

00:03:52.579 --> 00:03:59.479
was prompted by feeling some amount of existential
distress that has been on slow burn for a

00:03:59.479 --> 00:04:00.569
while now.

00:04:00.569 --> 00:04:03.869
And we don't have to start here in terms of
our conversation, but I want to mention it

00:04:03.869 --> 00:04:11.530
to folks, just to underscore the fact that
I do have you on the speed dial, per se, because

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:14.430
of your deep expertise and awareness related
to certain things.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:22.389
And in this case, it was angst and distress
related to climate change, extreme weather

00:04:22.389 --> 00:04:31.409
events and noticing a growing apathy, feeling
of learned helplessness perhaps, in my audience,

00:04:31.409 --> 00:04:33.720
which scares me more than most things.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:35.169
I mean, there's a lot of strife.

00:04:35.169 --> 00:04:36.229
There's a lot of anger.

00:04:36.229 --> 00:04:42.710
And I think these things may be intertwined
and we'll probably cover all of these in our

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:43.710
conversation.

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:48.900
Now, just to set the set the stage for this
uplifting conversation, before we started

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.400
recording, I asked what we might chat about.

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:54.131
And you said, "Well, death, war, and anger."

00:04:54.131 --> 00:04:57.820
So we will cover —
Jack Kornfield: All the cheerful topics.

00:04:57.820 --> 00:04:59.470
Tim Ferriss: All the cheerful topics.

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:05.310
Jack Kornfield: And joy and well-being, but
we have to make the descent and then come

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:08.040
back out reborn into a new brighter world.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:09.040
Tim Ferriss: Exactly.

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:14.740
So let's start with anger because in between
my rainmaking activities in the dry alpine

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:22.000
west, I also make time for stupid arguments
over breakfast, one of which I had this morning.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.750
I think that was also precipitated by yours
truly.

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:27.219
So not all great things.

00:05:27.219 --> 00:05:32.690
And you mentioned you have a number of stories
before we started recording, related to anger.

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:36.889
So let's start there and I'll let you lead
wherever you think it makes sense.

00:05:36.889 --> 00:05:38.050
Jack Kornfield: I will.

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:40.449
But let me ask you a question first.

00:05:40.449 --> 00:05:43.240
Why do you ask about anger?

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:46.680
What is your intention, or what makes you
ask?

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:55.630
Tim Ferriss: Well, I ask about anger because
I notice as I reflect on not just my own behavior,

00:05:55.630 --> 00:06:01.729
but behavior that is common in my family,
a tendency towards perhaps an emotional home

00:06:01.729 --> 00:06:03.460
base of anger.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:11.120
It's a very familiar feeling and I recognize
how it can be corrosive.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:17.499
And I've spoken to people who have wanted
to discuss their respective perspectives on

00:06:17.499 --> 00:06:19.470
say clean anger.

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:23.550
I have tried to contend with it in different
ways.

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:29.379
Some have suggested fully expressing it, hitting
a pillow, working on a punching bag.

00:06:29.379 --> 00:06:33.380
That tends to amplify and perpetuate anger
in me.

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:37.719
So it does not have the desired effect of
dissipating anger at all.

00:06:37.719 --> 00:06:42.120
It just turns it from a seven to a 10.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:48.639
And it's something I want to work on because
I also recognize that even when I convey neutral

00:06:48.639 --> 00:06:55.009
content, from the perspective of the words
that I choose, sometimes my tone has an undercurrent

00:06:55.009 --> 00:06:57.469
of anger and that causes strife.

00:06:57.469 --> 00:06:59.050
That causes arguments.

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:02.870
That causes various types of tension.

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:06.750
And a lot of it, most of it is not helpful.

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:08.830
So that is part of why I ask.

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:09.830
Jack Kornfield: Well, yeah.

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:12.729
Tim Ferriss: And If I may just add one more
thing.

00:07:12.729 --> 00:07:21.419
From a macro societal perspective, I have
never seen more polarization and anger than

00:07:21.419 --> 00:07:26.490
I see today, just observing this audience
of let's call it 10 to 20 million people a

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:27.490
month.

00:07:27.490 --> 00:07:32.949
As I look at all of it from a 30,000 foot
view, I have never seen so much anger and

00:07:32.949 --> 00:07:38.379
hostility ever in all my time on this planet,
so that's also frightening.

00:07:38.379 --> 00:07:44.800
Jack Kornfield: So that really helps me a
lot because your openness about it is actually

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:46.039
what we need.

00:07:46.039 --> 00:07:49.530
If we bury it or stuff it, it just festers.

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:53.830
As you say, you can try to be nice and then
it seeps out in the tone of your voice.

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:55.919
And people feel that.

00:07:55.919 --> 00:07:57.499
We resonate with one another.

00:07:57.499 --> 00:07:58.779
It's not a complicated thing.

00:07:58.779 --> 00:08:02.559
You could call it mystical, but if you put
a violin on the table and somebody else plays

00:08:02.559 --> 00:08:06.830
a violin nearby, the strings of both of them
will resonate.

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:09.510
And so yes, there you're looking so nice.

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:12.389
And then that little stuff seeps out.

00:08:12.389 --> 00:08:16.969
So I've really had to wrestle with anger a
lot.

00:08:16.969 --> 00:08:22.330
And the Greeks call it a noble emotion, the
philosophers, because there's something of

00:08:22.330 --> 00:08:23.330
value in it.

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:27.580
So the first thing I want to say is that anger
in itself isn't bad.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:32.130
It's difficult to work with, but it's not
a bad thing.

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:35.830
It's something for us to understand because
it's part of human incarnation.

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:43.260
And often the anger comes out of hurt, or
it comes out of fear, ways that we feel vulnerable.

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:45.110
It's a way of protecting ourself.

00:08:45.110 --> 00:08:51.500
James Baldwin said, he said, "I believe the
reason that people cling to their hate and

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:58.839
anger so stubbornly is because they're afraid
that once hate is gone, they'll be forced

00:08:58.839 --> 00:09:02.139
to face their own pain."

00:09:02.139 --> 00:09:09.190
And so we also project it on others, the ones
that cause our pain and so forth, instead

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:13.440
we get angry as if they were the ones making
it happen.

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:15.000
And we have the enemy du jour.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:16.000
It doesn't matter.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:19.500
Right now it's Russia and everybody's cheering.

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:20.760
We have the Pentagon.

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:22.990
It's like, I don't know what to say.

00:09:22.990 --> 00:09:23.990
They're celebrating.

00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:29.029
We have a great enemy again and a bigger budget
and so forth, but it can be the immigrants

00:09:29.029 --> 00:09:35.220
or the Mexicans or the blacks or the gays
or whoever it is, the enemy du jour.

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:41.860
And because we can't bear our insecurity and
we can't bear the fact that we're vulnerable

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:45.600
in certain ways and that what's out there
is also part of us.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:52.120
So Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn, the great Russian
author, when he was in the gulags, writing

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:59.050
these brilliant things and sent out into Siberia,
he said, "If only it were simple, if only

00:09:59.050 --> 00:10:03.899
we could just round up the evil people and
get rid of them.

00:10:03.899 --> 00:10:10.170
But the problem is the line dividing good
and evil cuts through the heart of every human

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:17.860
being, and who among us is willing to get
rid of a piece of our own heart?"

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:20.350
So this is where we are as human beings.

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:23.030
So here I am, I'm in the monastery.

00:10:23.030 --> 00:10:26.730
I did some years training as a Buddhist monk.

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:30.190
And in my family, there were four boys and
we each had different roles.

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:31.670
My brother got angry a lot.

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:36.880
I think that was the healthiest in some way,
because my father was an angry, paranoid wife

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:43.339
batterer, somewhat mentally ill, and also
a brilliant scientist who helped develop some

00:10:43.339 --> 00:10:48.480
of the first artificial hearts and lungs and
did space medicine and stuff.

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:52.650
But he was a terrifying figure in many ways.

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:56.449
So I was the peacemaker in the family, trying
to calm it down.

00:10:56.449 --> 00:10:58.589
I've now made that a profession.

00:10:58.589 --> 00:11:00.370
You know how that goes.

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:01.589
But anyway.

00:11:01.589 --> 00:11:06.170
And I go in the monastery and I'm sitting,
minding my own business, meditating, walking

00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:11.180
slowly in the forest in the remote part of
Thailand on the border of Laos.

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:13.680
That's an ascetic forest monastery.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:14.910
I wanted something tough.

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:15.910
You know?

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:16.910
We're young men.

00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:18.029
Is there anything dangerous to do around here?

00:11:18.029 --> 00:11:20.230
Let me try it.

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:26.279
And I start to sit and at some point, not
that long in my time there, the first months,

00:11:26.279 --> 00:11:30.740
I start to get more and more irritated at
the way the monks around me are behaving and

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:34.440
the problems and this one and that one.

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:37.800
And then I just notice that I'm getting angrier.

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:43.480
And all of a sudden I realize that I have
a lot of anger.

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:48.100
It just starts boiling up because there's
no distraction and I can't put it on somebody

00:11:48.100 --> 00:11:50.730
else really.

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:54.070
And so I go to talk to my teacher about it.

00:11:54.070 --> 00:11:57.050
I said, "All this anger was bubbling up."

00:11:57.050 --> 00:12:01.150
And basically it shocked me because I thought
I was a peaceful person.

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:04.930
And mostly, I just didn't want to be like
my father and hurt people.

00:12:04.930 --> 00:12:06.140
So I'm not going to be like him.

00:12:06.140 --> 00:12:09.740
But it turns out he's in there and it's all
in there.

00:12:09.740 --> 00:12:16.639
And I tell Ajahn Chah, my teacher, and he
looks at me and he says, "Good."

00:12:16.639 --> 00:12:17.980
He said, "Great.

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:19.390
This is what you need to look at."

00:12:19.390 --> 00:12:25.079
He said, "I want you to go back to your little
hut, had a tin roof, close the one window

00:12:25.079 --> 00:12:26.819
in door, put on all your robes.

00:12:26.819 --> 00:12:28.279
This is the hot season.

00:12:28.279 --> 00:12:30.269
And if you're going to be angry, do it right."

00:12:30.269 --> 00:12:32.029
He didn't say "Go beat something."

00:12:32.029 --> 00:12:37.329
He just said, "Sit in the middle of it, take
the seat and feel the fire of it.

00:12:37.329 --> 00:12:40.899
And first of all, learn to tolerate it."

00:12:40.899 --> 00:12:46.100
Because part of what happens with anger is
when we can't tolerate the emotions and so

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:49.050
forth, then we lash out, or it leaks in our
words.

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:55.660
And he said, "So go and sit and bring your
mindfulness to it and learn to tolerate it."

00:12:55.660 --> 00:13:01.730
Because a lot of the power of meditation as
the neuroscientists say, is to increase the

00:13:01.730 --> 00:13:03.670
window of tolerance.

00:13:03.670 --> 00:13:09.279
So you don't get reactive to things, but you
have the ability to feel things fully and

00:13:09.279 --> 00:13:10.870
then decide how to express it.

00:13:10.870 --> 00:13:16.890
So I sit there and after a while it's like,
okay, anger comes.

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:23.850
And the great image in the Buddhist teaching
is of the Buddha seated under the tree of

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:26.529
enlightenment, that big world story or myth.

00:13:26.529 --> 00:13:29.740
And while he is sitting there, he gets attacked
by the armies of Mara.

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:32.360
"You have no right to sit here."

00:13:32.360 --> 00:13:34.190
Mara comes as doubt.

00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:39.100
Mara brings temptations, and then it was all
the beautiful maidens and a golden chariot.

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:40.449
You could have all these things.

00:13:40.449 --> 00:13:41.449
The Buddha says, "No.

00:13:41.449 --> 00:13:42.569
Been there, done that.

00:13:42.569 --> 00:13:44.930
I want something freer than that."

00:13:44.930 --> 00:13:49.889
And then Mara attacks him with spears and
flaming arrows and things.

00:13:49.889 --> 00:13:55.829
And the Buddha's response is to say, "I see
you, Mara."

00:13:55.829 --> 00:13:59.260
Mara comes back other times in Buddha's life.

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:04.399
And the main thing that the Buddha does, he
doesn't fight Mara, he says, "I see who you

00:14:04.399 --> 00:14:05.399
are.

00:14:05.399 --> 00:14:06.399
I see you."

00:14:06.399 --> 00:14:12.100
And in that you can feel that there's a freedom
in the heart, a spaciousness to be present

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:13.100
for it.

00:14:13.100 --> 00:14:15.020
So I get better with this shit, right?

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:16.020
I'm okay.

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:18.019
Now I'm not quite so angry.

00:14:18.019 --> 00:14:19.240
I know I have it.

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:20.949
I leave the monastery.

00:14:20.949 --> 00:14:30.360
After some years, I go back to New England
where I'd been last, hook up with an old girlfriend

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:31.600
who was great.

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:36.319
She was doing graduate teaching at Harvard.

00:14:36.319 --> 00:14:42.089
And very quickly we get into conflict and
I realize, okay, it's one thing to deal with

00:14:42.089 --> 00:14:44.920
anger in myself.

00:14:44.920 --> 00:14:50.760
But being a monk, I had no training in how
to deal with relationship.

00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:51.910
You know?

00:14:51.910 --> 00:14:55.649
And going back was hard.

00:14:55.649 --> 00:14:58.449
People talk about leaving the monastery and
going back to their family.

00:14:58.449 --> 00:15:02.680
They forget that both Jesus and Buddha had
a hard time when they went home to their family.

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:03.680
Right?

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:09.800
So this is like the real place of — so I'm
there with this woman and all my neuroses

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:16.290
come up, my neediness, my whatever, fears,
all the things around intimacy that I didn't

00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:18.000
have to deal with as a monk.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:24.260
So I start to do that and I realize the anger
is building up again.

00:15:24.260 --> 00:15:25.510
She was great, actually.

00:15:25.510 --> 00:15:28.709
She said, "Okay, you dealt with it in yourself.

00:15:28.709 --> 00:15:30.730
Now let's do this together."

00:15:30.730 --> 00:15:31.829
So she says, "Come with me."

00:15:31.829 --> 00:15:33.089
She had an old Volvo station wagon.

00:15:33.089 --> 00:15:39.500
And in the back she had 10 cases, big cases
of bottles.

00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:41.750
And she said, "Come with me."

00:15:41.750 --> 00:15:45.760
She drove me to an old place where they were
demolishing a brick building.

00:15:45.760 --> 00:15:47.509
She said, "All right, get out.

00:15:47.509 --> 00:15:54.459
I want you to throw those suckers at the wall,
and say whatever you have [to say] about me,

00:15:54.459 --> 00:15:55.459
whatever.

00:15:55.459 --> 00:15:56.459
Just let it out."

00:15:56.459 --> 00:15:59.620
Not so much as pounding the bag, but just
making it all conscious.

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:01.439
It wasn't like I have to do that every day.

00:16:01.439 --> 00:16:07.310
And then I went into a Reichian therapy and
this guy said, "You have the monk's defense.

00:16:07.310 --> 00:16:12.649
You can be aware of anything, but you're afraid
to actually express it."

00:16:12.649 --> 00:16:14.240
So he said, "What —
Tim Ferriss: Jack, may I interrupt for a second?

00:16:14.240 --> 00:16:15.240
Jack Kornfield: Yeah.

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:16.240
Tim Ferriss: What type of therapist was that?

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:21.560
Jack Kornfield: He worked with Wilhelm Reich,
who was a contemporary right after Freud,

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:24.810
who worked with the energy and sexuality of
the body.

00:16:24.810 --> 00:16:30.330
And he asked me, "What time of day is hardest
for you?"

00:16:30.330 --> 00:16:32.820
And I said, morning, I'm not a morning person."

00:16:32.820 --> 00:16:33.820
He said, "Great.

00:16:33.820 --> 00:16:36.939
I'll see you at 6:00 a.m. when your defenses
are down."

00:16:36.939 --> 00:16:42.779
So I go in and he has me do these breathing
exercises to charge you up my body, play some

00:16:42.779 --> 00:16:48.079
evocative music and starts working on my body
saying, "You've got to let this stuff out,

00:16:48.079 --> 00:16:49.689
whatever it is."

00:16:49.689 --> 00:16:50.689
Okay.

00:16:50.689 --> 00:16:51.689
He worked on my body so hard.

00:16:51.689 --> 00:16:53.440
One day he cracked two ribs, actually.

00:16:53.440 --> 00:16:55.540
Tim Ferriss: Oh, geez.

00:16:55.540 --> 00:16:57.690
Jack Kornfield: I wept, I cried.

00:16:57.690 --> 00:16:58.690
I got angry.

00:16:58.690 --> 00:16:59.690
I did all these things.

00:16:59.690 --> 00:17:02.959
So this was part of my learning it.

00:17:02.959 --> 00:17:05.510
And now I'm a lot less afraid of it.

00:17:05.510 --> 00:17:07.569
And therefore I also can use it.

00:17:07.569 --> 00:17:12.350
So when I had my house being rebuilt and we
were supposed to go to Europe and the contractor

00:17:12.350 --> 00:17:17.250
who was a good guy, kept saying, "We're a
little behind," I said, "You got to finish.

00:17:17.250 --> 00:17:18.800
Before we travel for a long time."

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:20.130
Was putting it off.

00:17:20.130 --> 00:17:22.651
And I kept asking, nice guy that I am.

00:17:22.651 --> 00:17:23.930
And he says, "Oh."

00:17:23.930 --> 00:17:27.960
Finally, I said, "We've only got a month to
go and…"

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:29.220
And I yelled at him.

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:32.810
I said, "Man, if you don't finish this, I'm
going to sue your ass in court.

00:17:32.810 --> 00:17:34.000
I'm going to get it.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:35.540
I'm just tired of this shit."

00:17:35.540 --> 00:17:37.130
And I'm really angry.

00:17:37.130 --> 00:17:40.970
And he looks at me and says, "Oh, you really
want it done, don't you?"

00:17:40.970 --> 00:17:41.970
And I said, "Yeah."

00:17:41.970 --> 00:17:43.770
Next day, he sent like a triple crew.

00:17:43.770 --> 00:17:47.100
And I realized that I simply needed to speak
contractor-ese.

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:52.380
I was kind of speaking like Buddhist monk,
but that was not his language.

00:17:52.380 --> 00:18:00.520
So it became easier in some way to work with
it and to realize that I have my own inner

00:18:00.520 --> 00:18:01.520
Taliban.

00:18:01.520 --> 00:18:05.690
I have my own inner Iron Curtain, that it's
not just out there.

00:18:05.690 --> 00:18:13.031
And by taking this energy and saying, "Yes,
this is something that I can work with," it

00:18:13.031 --> 00:18:15.020
just becomes much easier for me.

00:18:15.020 --> 00:18:16.120
And it runs through me quicker.

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:17.510
It's not that it doesn't.

00:18:17.510 --> 00:18:20.950
I mean, I watched Thich Nhat Hanh or the Dalai
Lama get angry or upset.

00:18:20.950 --> 00:18:24.090
So it's not like a — it's part of us.

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:34.050
And then I guess the last thing to say, because
it also allows for a kind of graciousness

00:18:34.050 --> 00:18:35.780
about it.

00:18:35.780 --> 00:18:43.130
When I see myself getting angry, if you really
look, when I really look, underneath anger

00:18:43.130 --> 00:18:49.490
is hurt or fear or some kind of humiliation
or something like that, where you feel like

00:18:49.490 --> 00:18:50.770
you've been disrespected.

00:18:50.770 --> 00:18:56.020
And if I can drop from the anger to say, "What's
under this?"

00:18:56.020 --> 00:18:59.950
And then express it and say, "I really feel
hurt when you say that," or, "That makes me

00:18:59.950 --> 00:19:09.300
afraid," that very conflict with another person
or a group of people, it changes from being,

00:19:09.300 --> 00:19:14.090
"I'm right and you're wrong" to "This hurts,"
or "I'm afraid about this.'

00:19:14.090 --> 00:19:17.690
And we can have a genuine conversation that's
entirely different.

00:19:17.690 --> 00:19:20.720
So I'll pause here because that's a lot of
story.

00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:22.560
Tim Ferriss: That is.

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:27.620
And I want to dive right back into the stories
and your teacher's name —

00:19:27.620 --> 00:19:29.280
Jack Kornfield: Ajahn Chah.

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:30.950
Tim Ferriss: — Ajahn Chah.

00:19:30.950 --> 00:19:32.200
Jack Kornfield: Right.

00:19:32.200 --> 00:19:37.530
Tim Ferriss: So I recall from looking at my
notes from an earlier conversation that we've

00:19:37.530 --> 00:19:44.350
had on this podcast, you tell the story of
being out with this teacher collecting alms,

00:19:44.350 --> 00:19:48.740
food, one day, when you saw big rock in the
middle of a rice paddy.

00:19:48.740 --> 00:19:50.840
Tell me if I get any of these details wrong.

00:19:50.840 --> 00:19:53.670
And Ajahn Chah asked, "Is that boulder heavy?"

00:19:53.670 --> 00:19:57.610
And a number of the young monks said, "Yes,
it is, master."

00:19:57.610 --> 00:19:59.830
And then he replied with, "Not if you don't
pick it up."

00:19:59.830 --> 00:20:00.830
Right?

00:20:00.830 --> 00:20:01.830
Jack Kornfield: Yeah.

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:04.560
Tim Ferriss: And being able to witness what's
present without being lost in it.

00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:12.420
Now let's contrast that for a second, because
I feel like you said, this internal Iron Curtain

00:20:12.420 --> 00:20:17.490
sometimes, or this internal conflict between
not picking up the boulder.

00:20:17.490 --> 00:20:18.490
Right?

00:20:18.490 --> 00:20:25.410
So not voluntarily overexerting and speaking
contractor-ese.

00:20:25.410 --> 00:20:30.360
So my question for you is, if you have the
impulse to speak con contractor-ese, where

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:33.570
you're just like, "For fuck's sake, enough
is enough.

00:20:33.570 --> 00:20:41.390
So and so isn't doing the job they're supposed
to do," how do you surgically use contractor-ese

00:20:41.390 --> 00:20:48.850
in your own life and know when to sort of
turn off Mr. Nice Guy and speak more directly?

00:20:48.850 --> 00:20:52.610
Because that I imagine can also become a hammer
looking for nails, right?

00:20:52.610 --> 00:20:53.610
Jack Kornfield: Yeah.

00:20:53.610 --> 00:20:57.560
Tim Ferriss: I think that's something that
I do, where someone might, excuse constantly

00:20:57.560 --> 00:21:01.810
speaking to everyone in contractor-ese with,
"Well, I'm just keeping it real," or, "I'm

00:21:01.810 --> 00:21:06.960
just being honest," or whatever the phrase
is that they use to deflect the fact that

00:21:06.960 --> 00:21:09.180
they're just being an asshole a lot of the
time.

00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:10.900
How do you think about that?

00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:15.440
Jack Kornfield: So I would, in a certain way,
I'd offer you a challenge because you like

00:21:15.440 --> 00:21:16.440
challenges.

00:21:16.440 --> 00:21:19.800
You're one of those guys that say, "Anything
dangerous to do around here?"

00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:21.470
But it's both outward and inward.

00:21:21.470 --> 00:21:28.030
And the challenge would be this, that when
you speak from that place, which I do, and

00:21:28.030 --> 00:21:32.580
it has its time, often, it makes things worse.

00:21:32.580 --> 00:21:38.020
I think Benjamin Franklin said, "What begins
in anger ends in suffering," generally speaking.

00:21:38.020 --> 00:21:45.250
So the challenge is, can you speak your truth
that's underneath the anger?

00:21:45.250 --> 00:21:50.800
Can you say, "I am worried that that isn't
going to happen, or that this is all going

00:21:50.800 --> 00:21:54.200
to fall apart, or I feel so hurt?"

00:21:54.200 --> 00:21:55.200
You know?

00:21:55.200 --> 00:21:56.460
When you say those kind of things.

00:21:56.460 --> 00:22:03.280
So when you do those things, can you actually
drop — now you're aware of your anger and

00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:09.670
allow a certain deeper level of understanding
and then express it.

00:22:09.670 --> 00:22:13.490
It doesn't mean that there might not be occasions
when you need to stand up.

00:22:13.490 --> 00:22:20.340
But when you talk about saying the truth,
Tim, the problem is that pesky little word

00:22:20.340 --> 00:22:21.340
"truth.”

00:22:21.340 --> 00:22:22.340
You know?

00:22:22.340 --> 00:22:27.430
Because there's a truth of how Tim sees it
and how it should be, but there's another

00:22:27.430 --> 00:22:32.270
truth that you feel worried, or hurt, or anxious,
and so forth.

00:22:32.270 --> 00:22:38.550
That's also true, and that's a different truth
that's even more challenging, sometimes, to

00:22:38.550 --> 00:22:43.280
express, but it connects you.

00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:50.040
And when people are arguing, I think about
in couples, you can say the simplest thing,

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:51.530
like, "What did you mean?"

00:22:51.530 --> 00:22:58.300
But with different tones of voice, like, "What
did you mean?" and it's like an attack, or

00:22:58.300 --> 00:23:00.500
you can say, "What did you mean?

00:23:00.500 --> 00:23:02.440
I really want to understand you."

00:23:02.440 --> 00:23:08.520
It's the very same words, but the feeling
that's communicated is either blame, or it's

00:23:08.520 --> 00:23:11.400
"I'm actually curious, what's going on in
you?

00:23:11.400 --> 00:23:13.010
What did you mean?"

00:23:13.010 --> 00:23:17.770
And so there's no simple answer, but it's
like riding a bike, yeah, you fall off each

00:23:17.770 --> 00:23:19.230
side, and you practice it.

00:23:19.230 --> 00:23:25.040
Tim Ferriss: So let me, once again, turn this
conversation into just a self-serving therapy

00:23:25.040 --> 00:23:31.490
session, because I think that it helps me,
it'll probably help, it will help at least

00:23:31.490 --> 00:23:34.740
a few other people out there, and I also don't
want to misrepresent myself.

00:23:34.740 --> 00:23:40.520
I'm not just raging, throwing rocks through
windows nonstop 24/7, I think maybe, you can

00:23:40.520 --> 00:23:44.300
correct me if I'm wrong, but I think it was
the Buddha who said, "If you walk outside

00:23:44.300 --> 00:23:48.660
and one day you meet one asshole, that person's
an asshole, but if you walk outside and everyone's

00:23:48.660 --> 00:23:50.050
an asshole, you're the asshole."

00:23:50.050 --> 00:23:54.280
I might be distributing that, it could have
been Abraham Lincoln or Oscar Wilde, but I

00:23:54.280 --> 00:23:55.290
do think about that.

00:23:55.290 --> 00:24:01.320
So I'm not raging, I'm not a rage-aholic,
I wouldn't say, but the biggest challenge

00:24:01.320 --> 00:24:08.280
I think that I have is not the words I use,
but the slight edge or tone that I think,

00:24:08.280 --> 00:24:14.760
whether by genetics or environment, I have
adopted, because there was a lot of this in

00:24:14.760 --> 00:24:17.630
my household.

00:24:17.630 --> 00:24:24.730
I'm unclear on how to systematically work
on catching that tone, and I have to imagine

00:24:24.730 --> 00:24:26.450
it begins with awareness.

00:24:26.450 --> 00:24:33.230
Now retrospectively I'm aware, for instance,
this morning, the issue wasn't what I said,

00:24:33.230 --> 00:24:36.890
it was probably the edge with which I said
it.

00:24:36.890 --> 00:24:41.320
Do you have any suggestions for how to work
on that?

00:24:41.320 --> 00:24:46.530
Jack Kornfield: Well, it's a deep and a beautiful
question for us as human beings, because it

00:24:46.530 --> 00:24:53.890
can be anger or it can be other patterns,
addictive patterns, or other things that we

00:24:53.890 --> 00:24:55.250
fall into.

00:24:55.250 --> 00:25:00.110
And you said something straight up right away,
the first step is just to be aware of it,

00:25:00.110 --> 00:25:03.500
because if you're not aware of it, you're
on automatic pilot.

00:25:03.500 --> 00:25:09.740
The next step is, you said I'm aware of it
retrospectively, you want to move the time

00:25:09.740 --> 00:25:13.250
a little closer to when it happens.

00:25:13.250 --> 00:25:19.110
And there are two things you can do, one is
pause, start to tune yourself, you could even

00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:20.320
take a little notebook.

00:25:20.320 --> 00:25:24.840
I think it's a great thing anyway, get a little
notebook and put the word anger on the first

00:25:24.840 --> 00:25:28.700
page, and then just periodically let yourself
write about it.

00:25:28.700 --> 00:25:34.100
So you let images of your family, anger about
the world, the climate, whatever, you just

00:25:34.100 --> 00:25:38.170
let it, so you start to see, okay, this is
the repository of angry, this is what I'm

00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:43.830
learning about it, this is when it comes,
so you're making it more conscious.

00:25:43.830 --> 00:25:52.170
Then as you catch up to it more in the current
moment when it arises, the next step is to

00:25:52.170 --> 00:26:00.950
pause, and with your notebook, if you write
down little examples, you don't have to fill

00:26:00.950 --> 00:26:06.500
it with pages, but all right, this morning,
tone of voice I got, then you can begin to

00:26:06.500 --> 00:26:10.400
imagine or feel a different scenario.

00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:16.410
Don't even do it out loud yet, but imagine
what upset you in the morning, even with that

00:26:16.410 --> 00:26:20.130
little tone you'd say, how else could I have
handled this?

00:26:20.130 --> 00:26:21.130
What else?

00:26:21.130 --> 00:26:26.830
And the thing is that the imagination has
an enormous capacity to change our neural

00:26:26.830 --> 00:26:34.000
structure, how we envision, as you know Olympic
athletes, they envision and they go into a

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:38.090
game and they do better, you know this very
well, you actually get to envision yourself,

00:26:38.090 --> 00:26:43.020
all right, now I am the Buddha, I have a certain
fierce compassion, so you haven't put it all

00:26:43.020 --> 00:26:50.160
aside, but basically I'm a gracious and caring
human being, how would that come out in this

00:26:50.160 --> 00:26:51.340
situation?

00:26:51.340 --> 00:26:57.260
And you start to realize that you have this
capacity, if you really begin to look at it,

00:26:57.260 --> 00:27:02.820
to transform your patterns, which is what,
that's why it's called practice rather than

00:27:02.820 --> 00:27:05.620
perfect, whether it's meditation practice
or something else.

00:27:05.620 --> 00:27:07.860
And I — well, there are two things.

00:27:07.860 --> 00:27:14.360
There's a word, querencia, in Spanish, and
it refers to the place in bullfighting where

00:27:14.360 --> 00:27:20.200
the bull stops in the middle of the ring at
some point, with the bullfighter there, and

00:27:20.200 --> 00:27:23.900
the bull and the matador are facing off.

00:27:23.900 --> 00:27:32.870
And the matador finds his querencia, it's
the place in the middle of all of that energy

00:27:32.870 --> 00:27:39.770
which is unmoving, which is just here I am,
on the earth, there's the bull, there's all

00:27:39.770 --> 00:27:41.270
of this stuff.

00:27:41.270 --> 00:27:50.480
If you find that in yourself when you practice,
when you learn to find that, then it gives

00:27:50.480 --> 00:27:52.480
you all kinds of other opportunities.

00:27:52.480 --> 00:27:56.710
And I remember this woman coming to see me
who was in the middle of a terrible divorce,

00:27:56.710 --> 00:27:59.170
so we're talking real things here.

00:27:59.170 --> 00:28:06.730
Her ex-husband, who is also a wealthy lawyer,
hired a killer lawyer, he was going to keep

00:28:06.730 --> 00:28:10.720
the kids and keep all the money, he was angry
at her, even though he's the one that had

00:28:10.720 --> 00:28:11.980
the affairs.

00:28:11.980 --> 00:28:18.660
But anyway, so I said, "First of all, get
yourself a kickass lawyer to defend yourself,"

00:28:18.660 --> 00:28:23.260
read that from the Buddha, I don't know, anyway,
wherever that comes from.

00:28:23.260 --> 00:28:27.770
But we worked for a while and she came in
one day, because he was trying to turn their

00:28:27.770 --> 00:28:36.640
children against her, Which is one of the
greatest pains a parent can have, but also

00:28:36.640 --> 00:28:38.720
not so uncommon.

00:28:38.720 --> 00:28:44.720
And she said, "I sat with it, I let myself
feel all the pain, and the tremendous amount

00:28:44.720 --> 00:28:48.930
of grief," and that's part of our work, was
just the grief of it.

00:28:48.930 --> 00:28:53.790
And then she came in one day and she said,
"I will not bequeath a legacy of bitterness

00:28:53.790 --> 00:28:55.490
to my children.

00:28:55.490 --> 00:29:00.120
I will not say a bad thing about their father,
I simply will not do it."

00:29:00.120 --> 00:29:05.360
And that was the querencia, that was "It doesn't
matter what the bull does, I know what my

00:29:05.360 --> 00:29:08.270
values are, and I know who I am."

00:29:08.270 --> 00:29:12.750
And that, you have that in yourself.

00:29:12.750 --> 00:29:14.690
That power is in you.

00:29:14.690 --> 00:29:26.100
Tim Ferriss: So Jack, I want to make sure
that we talk about death, and from the notes

00:29:26.100 --> 00:29:30.670
that we exchanged before we started recording,
what happens as you die, or when you die.

00:29:30.670 --> 00:29:34.960
But before we get to that, to give people
something to look forward to, I wanted to

00:29:34.960 --> 00:29:39.470
chat with you a bit about something you and
I haven't spent that much time talking about

00:29:39.470 --> 00:29:50.830
offline, and I feel like this may connect
closely to my anger response, or just general

00:29:50.830 --> 00:29:54.060
aggressive response to the world, and that
is hypervigilance.

00:29:54.060 --> 00:30:01.790
So you know about my childhood trauma and
my traumatic background, per se.

00:30:01.790 --> 00:30:05.780
For people who want more on that just search
my name and trauma and it'll pop right up,

00:30:05.780 --> 00:30:07.450
so we won't get into it right now.

00:30:07.450 --> 00:30:16.640
But there's a degree of hypervigilance in
always seeking safety and maximizing, or optimizing,

00:30:16.640 --> 00:30:27.620
for safety that I think may be the foundation
under which a lot of this anger arises, and

00:30:27.620 --> 00:30:35.060
I'm wondering how you have worked with people,
whether they have complex PTSD, or undiagnosed

00:30:35.060 --> 00:30:40.200
traumatic response in the form of hypervigilance,
how do you work on that with people?

00:30:40.200 --> 00:30:46.760
Jack Kornfield: Well first of all I'm just
listening to you and feeling the suffering

00:30:46.760 --> 00:30:54.490
of it, not just for you, but for the child
that you were, and for the child of so many

00:30:54.490 --> 00:30:59.140
people who are listening, the childhood, not
everybody's, but I became somewhat that way,

00:30:59.140 --> 00:31:05.220
not perhaps as much as you, because I didn't
know when the car pulled in the driveway whether

00:31:05.220 --> 00:31:12.510
I going to get a raging violent maniac father,
or just a ordinary father.

00:31:12.510 --> 00:31:17.960
So I had to be vigilant like you.

00:31:17.960 --> 00:31:22.190
Underneath that there's fear, but there's
also a lot of grief.

00:31:22.190 --> 00:31:31.360
The loss of what could have been a healthier
family, the grief that that child wasn't protected,

00:31:31.360 --> 00:31:33.520
and so forth.

00:31:33.520 --> 00:31:42.440
So for me part of what helps with the vigilance
is bringing a kind of compassion to that frightened

00:31:42.440 --> 00:31:48.500
part of ourselves, because it's not just you
as an adult, but as you point out, it's really

00:31:48.500 --> 00:31:53.900
that hypervigilance was learned long ago to
protect you.

00:31:53.900 --> 00:32:01.200
And so I will do a practice of having people
visualize themself when they had to learn

00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:07.850
that, when they weren't safe, do some compassion
practice, put their arm around that child,

00:32:07.850 --> 00:32:20.400
hold that part of themself, really, in some
way, and also begin to say to all that fear

00:32:20.400 --> 00:32:26.480
almost a mantra recitation, "Thank you for
trying to protect me," because it wires in

00:32:26.480 --> 00:32:32.250
the fight, flight, or freeze immediately in
the brain, the tiger's coming, or whatever.

00:32:32.250 --> 00:32:36.020
"Thank you for trying to protect me," you
feel your vulnerability.

00:32:36.020 --> 00:32:42.370
"I'm okay just now, I'm all right, thank you,
you can relax."

00:32:42.370 --> 00:32:48.020
And it becomes actually a practice where you
let yourself feel the hypervigilance and you

00:32:48.020 --> 00:32:51.730
say, "Thank you for trying to protect me,"
so you're not suppressing it, you actually

00:32:51.730 --> 00:32:55.230
honor it, because it kept you safe.

00:32:55.230 --> 00:32:59.650
And you say, "And right now I'm okay," and
then you let yourself feel it in your body.

00:32:59.650 --> 00:33:05.080
"I actually am okay in this moment, I'm safe,
thank you, I'll be all right."

00:33:05.080 --> 00:33:13.750
And again, it becomes a training that you
do where you are acknowledging what's true,

00:33:13.750 --> 00:33:20.350
the suffering of it, the activation, and the
grief that's underneath it, the tears that

00:33:20.350 --> 00:33:22.660
are under it.

00:33:22.660 --> 00:33:26.560
And now we're kind of also in the territory
of trauma which you and I have talked about

00:33:26.560 --> 00:33:32.840
before, and it's so important in this time.

00:33:32.840 --> 00:33:41.830
I might have, I don't know, in that last conversation
we had, in the podcast, I probably referred

00:33:41.830 --> 00:33:48.730
to some of the retreats that we did, Michael
Meade, Luis Rodriguez, other compadres of

00:33:48.730 --> 00:33:56.130
mine, compatriots, with returning vets from
the Middle East, who have tremendous amount

00:33:56.130 --> 00:34:04.760
of trauma, and part of what was needed was
to make a safe enough space that they could

00:34:04.760 --> 00:34:13.720
bring out, in a conscious way, that which
was kept inside out of fear, to protect themselves

00:34:13.720 --> 00:34:14.919
and others.

00:34:14.919 --> 00:34:21.980
So we got combat vets together, and part of
it was to write, or talk, or tell your story.

00:34:21.980 --> 00:34:25.559
And these are stories that they couldn't tell
anybody else.

00:34:25.559 --> 00:34:33.889
You can't go home and tell people the graphic
details of what happened in the battle zone;

00:34:33.889 --> 00:34:36.320
it would shatter them.

00:34:36.320 --> 00:34:47.059
I know it also, working with physicians and
nurses in neonatal units in the hospital and

00:34:47.059 --> 00:34:53.290
my beloved Trudy, who has done that work,
talks about how the nurses come home and they

00:34:53.290 --> 00:34:59.160
can't tell the people around them that, "This
week I held three babies in my arms as they

00:34:59.160 --> 00:35:00.960
died," and describe it, you can't do it.

00:35:00.960 --> 00:35:05.420
So the vets say, "I can't, I can't," and this
was the powerful thing, "I can't tell you

00:35:05.420 --> 00:35:15.760
what I saw, and I can't tell you what I had
to do," and that's the worst, because it's

00:35:15.760 --> 00:35:19.829
a weight on the soul of a vet.

00:35:19.829 --> 00:35:25.640
So first we got them together, and as combat
vets they found a safe space to tell their

00:35:25.640 --> 00:35:30.290
stories that they couldn't tell to anybody
else.

00:35:30.290 --> 00:35:37.390
And we also invited them to write a story,
or write a poem, or begin to articulate it,

00:35:37.390 --> 00:35:44.579
and it's held in the body, so there was movement
that we did, and martial art kind of things.

00:35:44.579 --> 00:35:53.759
There was some group songs, the songs of warriors
returning from Africa, or Tibet, the Mayan,

00:35:53.759 --> 00:35:58.760
that we could chant together, and the stories
of what it meant for a warrior to put down

00:35:58.760 --> 00:36:05.079
their armor and come home and become, again,
part of those who till the fields and care

00:36:05.079 --> 00:36:10.029
for their families, and there are all these
ancient myths about it, and people began to

00:36:10.029 --> 00:36:12.779
let all that out that they were carrying.

00:36:12.779 --> 00:36:22.020
And then in the end we invited their family
to come in, and each man stood up and told

00:36:22.020 --> 00:36:27.380
at least some part of the story that they'd
never told before.

00:36:27.380 --> 00:36:36.279
And as each person spoke, when it was done
there was a group chant of honoring them and

00:36:36.279 --> 00:36:42.440
saying, "We now welcome you back, a warrior,
who've done your deeds as a warrior.

00:36:42.440 --> 00:36:48.080
You can let that go now, and we welcome you
back as a human being in a new way in our

00:36:48.080 --> 00:36:49.509
midst."

00:36:49.509 --> 00:36:57.300
I mean, otherwise people get out of the military
and the bus stops at the corner, or whatever,

00:36:57.300 --> 00:37:01.579
and they're left out and nobody knows, nobody
tends them.

00:37:01.579 --> 00:37:11.059
So I tell the story partly because it's important
in terms of trauma that it's held in our body,

00:37:11.059 --> 00:37:18.119
we need to be able to tell the story, we need
others also around us to witness us and to

00:37:18.119 --> 00:37:24.140
see that this isn't who you really are, this
was a role you took on.

00:37:24.140 --> 00:37:32.630
But it's not who you really are, and that
mirroring also has a tremendous benefit for

00:37:32.630 --> 00:37:37.490
blessing when you're seen in a different way,
because then you can start to see yourself

00:37:37.490 --> 00:37:40.299
in a different way.

00:37:40.299 --> 00:37:42.529
I digressed from your question.

00:37:42.529 --> 00:37:47.789
Tim Ferriss: No, I don't think it's a digression,
I think these are parallel tracks, and they're

00:37:47.789 --> 00:37:49.539
probably converging tracks at certain points.

00:37:49.539 --> 00:37:53.250
I realize those two don't go together, for
those people out there who are sticklers about

00:37:53.250 --> 00:38:00.380
parallel, but a question that comes to mind
for me also, which is very front and center,

00:38:00.380 --> 00:38:08.019
is I think about mental health, new therapeutics,
new models for trying to address mental health.

00:38:08.019 --> 00:38:18.410
The United States just as a country is woefully
underprepared for the sheer volume of patient

00:38:18.410 --> 00:38:20.640
cases that need to be dealt with.

00:38:20.640 --> 00:38:26.250
If you compare them to almost any other first
world country, whether it's a Scandinavian

00:38:26.250 --> 00:38:32.430
country, or even much larger countries, from
the perspective of having trained psychologists

00:38:32.430 --> 00:38:40.190
and psychiatrists, the US really has woefully
inadequate ratios, which leads me to wonder

00:38:40.190 --> 00:38:45.819
how you think about group therapy dynamics?

00:38:45.819 --> 00:38:52.900
Because it seems like one way to increase
the treatable population while decreasing

00:38:52.900 --> 00:38:59.210
the per person costs, and this applies, as
you just indicated, not just in some of the

00:38:59.210 --> 00:39:03.589
spheres that I know well, and you know extremely
well, but let's just say in the psychedelic

00:39:03.589 --> 00:39:09.900
therapeutics arena, where you could look at
group therapies and integration with some

00:39:09.900 --> 00:39:15.369
of these different compounds, whether it's
MDMA, or others say psilocybin for treatment

00:39:15.369 --> 00:39:21.990
resistant, or major depressive disorder, but
it applies also in un-enhanced environments,

00:39:21.990 --> 00:39:24.150
like the veteran example that you just provided.

00:39:24.150 --> 00:39:31.609
Do you have any thoughts on paths forward,
or paths worth exploring with respect to group

00:39:31.609 --> 00:39:32.619
therapeutic models?

00:39:32.619 --> 00:39:33.619
Jack Kornfield: I do.

00:39:33.619 --> 00:39:35.258
Tim Ferriss: Because it seems like it's not
just, I'm sorry to interrupt, to ask and then

00:39:35.258 --> 00:39:38.809
interrupt, it seems like it's not just a way
to, I want to emphasize in my mind, it's not

00:39:38.809 --> 00:39:44.800
just a way to decrease costs, it's also a
way to, in many, many instances, improve outcomes,

00:39:44.800 --> 00:39:47.460
rather than having one-on-one isolated treatments.

00:39:47.460 --> 00:39:48.990
Jack Kornfield: Yes.

00:39:48.990 --> 00:39:56.839
So first I want to note something else, we
have a mental health crisis here, and Vivek

00:39:56.839 --> 00:40:04.069
Murthy, who's the current Surgeon General,
and a great guy that I know, said what he's

00:40:04.069 --> 00:40:09.680
seen, more than half of what comes through
the doors of our hospitals and clinics presenting

00:40:09.680 --> 00:40:17.400
as physical problems really has its root in
emotions and mental, that it's the level of

00:40:17.400 --> 00:40:25.930
stress, and that's really part of the crisis,
and we're allowed to have a medical problem,

00:40:25.930 --> 00:40:28.320
but it's actually not tended.

00:40:28.320 --> 00:40:33.920
There was a section of The Wall Street Journal,
dozen years ago or so, it was the front page

00:40:33.920 --> 00:40:43.930
of it actually, and it talked about the overmedication
of children, front page Wall Street Journal,

00:40:43.930 --> 00:40:53.859
saying that 45 million of our children are
on antidepressants or ADHD medication, or

00:40:53.859 --> 00:41:03.180
antihypertensive, or antipsychotic, or various
medications like that.

00:41:03.180 --> 00:41:05.599
Huge number.

00:41:05.599 --> 00:41:08.749
And then we have to pause and say: our children,
why?

00:41:08.749 --> 00:41:16.359
Even before we get to your question of how
do we heal, there's something about the way

00:41:16.359 --> 00:41:27.130
our culture, the drivenness of our culture,
and the outer focus of it, in which we're

00:41:27.130 --> 00:41:29.210
actually not listening to our children.

00:41:29.210 --> 00:41:37.890
And you see it in all kinds of ways, I don't
even have to go any further, but even the

00:41:37.890 --> 00:41:43.119
terrible gun violence that we see, and so
forth, in some way comes out of a tremendous

00:41:43.119 --> 00:41:44.319
amount of pain in childhood.

00:41:44.319 --> 00:41:48.400
I'm not trying to make any excuse for that.

00:41:48.400 --> 00:41:52.289
So here we are, we're in a culture, now let's
just look at it clearly, we're a culture that

00:41:52.289 --> 00:41:57.499
doesn't how to do this, and I think it has
to happen on a lot of levels.

00:41:57.499 --> 00:42:02.980
You're correct that this isn't an individual
problem, and people will come to see me and

00:42:02.980 --> 00:42:08.960
say, "I have a tremendous amount of anxiety
right now, and I'm anxious about this, this,

00:42:08.960 --> 00:42:13.901
this in my life," and I look at them, I say,
"You may be anxious about that, but you're

00:42:13.901 --> 00:42:18.710
living in a culture that's diffused with anxiety
right now."

00:42:18.710 --> 00:42:24.320
It's not just the earth, you're actually swimming
in the psyche, or the field of consciousness

00:42:24.320 --> 00:42:28.420
around you, and it's in the news, and it's
what people talk about, and you can't not

00:42:28.420 --> 00:42:29.420
feel it.

00:42:29.420 --> 00:42:33.349
So it's not just personal, nor is it personal
to those children.

00:42:33.349 --> 00:42:37.529
And in some way stepping back and seeing that
already starts to give you a little relief,

00:42:37.529 --> 00:42:43.930
okay, it's not my fault, I'm not doing something
wrong, because we can so easily judge one

00:42:43.930 --> 00:42:47.849
another, or judge our mental state.

00:42:47.849 --> 00:42:57.150
So then, going to your very important question,
there are new modalities of healing, and certainly

00:42:57.150 --> 00:43:06.299
some of the psychedelic work with psilocybin,
ketamine, MDMA, tremendously helpful and promising.

00:43:06.299 --> 00:43:11.170
But the other part which you talk about, and
it's really the epidemic of loneliness, it's

00:43:11.170 --> 00:43:13.990
what the vets were feeling when they were
in their home alone and they couldn't talk

00:43:13.990 --> 00:43:20.000
to anybody about, all that was inside them,
is that we need each other.

00:43:20.000 --> 00:43:25.849
And healing is not an individual matter, for
almost everybody that is human being, especially

00:43:25.849 --> 00:43:30.079
the healing of the heart and the psyche.

00:43:30.079 --> 00:43:38.650
We need to hear each other, to comfort each
other, to allow the measure of grief, and

00:43:38.650 --> 00:43:43.549
with it then to allow something more beautiful
to arise, because if you could see the vets

00:43:43.549 --> 00:43:50.270
after that retreat, there was a tremendous
amount of love that grew that they could express,

00:43:50.270 --> 00:43:54.410
because they felt free in themselves in some
way.

00:43:54.410 --> 00:44:00.280
And so it's not just that you want to get
back to, okay, I can function again, but that

00:44:00.280 --> 00:44:06.109
you want to renew your heart, and you want
to renew your soul and your spirit, and that's

00:44:06.109 --> 00:44:07.109
possible.

00:44:07.109 --> 00:44:11.869
And usually it needs to be done together,
it can't only be done in yourself.

00:44:11.869 --> 00:44:20.450
Tim Ferriss: So Jack, let me ask you a question
about the anxiety-suffused society in which

00:44:20.450 --> 00:44:27.940
we find ourselves, and this is a question
I'm very interested in personally.

00:44:27.940 --> 00:44:38.350
So to what extent do you think about creating
the ideal environment for yourself and your

00:44:38.350 --> 00:44:49.170
loved ones, versus adjusting to an anxiety-suffused
society that is unlikely to be wholly changed

00:44:49.170 --> 00:44:50.170
by your efforts?

00:44:50.170 --> 00:44:56.590
And it makes me think of a very often quoted
quote of Krishnamurti, which is, "It is no

00:44:56.590 --> 00:44:59.980
measure of health to be well-adjusted to a
profoundly sick society."

00:44:59.980 --> 00:45:03.529
But one could argue that is absolving yourself
of any responsibility.

00:45:03.529 --> 00:45:11.710
So how do you think about choosing between
those two, or a ratio between those two, or

00:45:11.710 --> 00:45:16.710
contending with that tension if you see any
tension between those two?

00:45:16.710 --> 00:45:22.040
Jack Kornfield: I love your question, because
it's a little like a Zen koan, the world is

00:45:22.040 --> 00:45:24.480
on fire, it's inflamed.

00:45:24.480 --> 00:45:27.190
Do I stay around and try to put the fire out?

00:45:27.190 --> 00:45:32.150
Or the Titanic's going down, do I help people
in the lifeboats?

00:45:32.150 --> 00:45:33.150
Or do I flee?

00:45:33.150 --> 00:45:37.480
Do I go in the mountains, or do I get a little
lifeboat and so forth?

00:45:37.480 --> 00:45:39.609
Do I stay, or do I go?

00:45:39.609 --> 00:45:44.859
And the answer to those koans is "Yeah," which
you point to.

00:45:44.859 --> 00:45:46.690
You gave the little mathematical answer.

00:45:46.690 --> 00:45:49.369
How do you titrate it in some fashion?

00:45:49.369 --> 00:45:51.359
Because I think it's both.

00:45:51.359 --> 00:45:57.859
The metaphor that everyone uses these days
is, put on your oxygen mask first before you

00:45:57.859 --> 00:45:59.490
put it on your children.

00:45:59.490 --> 00:46:07.559
There's a way in which we have a responsibility
to ourselves to try to stay healthy and well.

00:46:07.559 --> 00:46:13.710
And I know if I go into a refugee camp, it
doesn't help if I go in and I'm depressed

00:46:13.710 --> 00:46:17.069
and anxious, they're already feeling upset
they don't need me to breathe.

00:46:17.069 --> 00:46:20.440
They actually need somebody who says, "Yeah,
this is suffering and they are ways to manage

00:46:20.440 --> 00:46:21.440
it.

00:46:21.440 --> 00:46:27.890
There are ways, even though it's difficult,
there are ways and there are possibilities

00:46:27.890 --> 00:46:28.990
for us."

00:46:28.990 --> 00:46:38.589
The great anthropologist and philosopher Wade
Davis said, "Despair is a failure of the imagination."

00:46:38.589 --> 00:46:41.240
Tim Ferriss: That's a good one.

00:46:41.240 --> 00:46:45.869
Jack Kornfield: Brilliant line, because it
speaks to the heart.

00:46:45.869 --> 00:46:49.569
Despair is a failure of the imagination.

00:46:49.569 --> 00:46:58.880
Human beings have been in tough times since
caves and saber tooth tigers, since battles

00:46:58.880 --> 00:47:00.420
between different clans.

00:47:00.420 --> 00:47:08.039
And then we don't have to talk about the Huns
and the Visegrads, or the Roman army, or the

00:47:08.039 --> 00:47:12.369
armies of the, whatever, the Aztecs, or wherever
around the world.

00:47:12.369 --> 00:47:20.930
We, human beings, have dealt with epidemics,
flood, tornadoes.

00:47:20.930 --> 00:47:24.490
Continuing war is not a new story, right?

00:47:24.490 --> 00:47:28.529
And we're survivors and we survive in two
ways.

00:47:28.529 --> 00:47:33.239
First of all, we survive by protecting what
we care about, which is ourselves and those

00:47:33.239 --> 00:47:34.239
close to us.

00:47:34.239 --> 00:47:35.239
So I do that.

00:47:35.239 --> 00:47:40.820
I want to protect my grandson, have him live
in a place where it's safe, if that's possible,

00:47:40.820 --> 00:47:46.550
or it's safe enough, or myself.

00:47:46.550 --> 00:47:50.930
But I also don't want to turn away for two
reasons.

00:47:50.930 --> 00:47:56.730
My daughter said, "Well, if climate change
and everything gets bad, would you move to

00:47:56.730 --> 00:47:58.299
New Zealand?

00:47:58.299 --> 00:48:00.569
Or would you get a..."

00:48:00.569 --> 00:48:07.170
And I said, "I choose to be on the deck of
the Titanic, and I'll help people in the lifeboats."

00:48:07.170 --> 00:48:12.980
That's what I've taken as a vow, as a Bodhisattva,
that you turn towards suffering, and you offer

00:48:12.980 --> 00:48:13.980
what you can.

00:48:13.980 --> 00:48:20.779
But part of that offering is to see that the
world that I want for my grandson needs my

00:48:20.779 --> 00:48:21.779
attending.

00:48:21.779 --> 00:48:27.059
And it doesn't mean I can change it, because
this is the way of the world in some ways,

00:48:27.059 --> 00:48:30.289
but it can be better or it can be worse and
we know that.

00:48:30.289 --> 00:48:32.910
We can stand up for things that really matter.

00:48:32.910 --> 00:48:37.460
And so your question, which is a really, it's
a question of the heart.

00:48:37.460 --> 00:48:39.849
It's also different for different people.

00:48:39.849 --> 00:48:42.300
There's some people who are profoundly contemplative.

00:48:42.300 --> 00:48:47.730
And I think the extreme are those Tibetan
yogis in a cave, who are broadcasting compassion

00:48:47.730 --> 00:48:49.440
to everybody in the world.

00:48:49.440 --> 00:48:56.849
If we're all connected, as they say, as we
find, then that's their contribution.

00:48:56.849 --> 00:49:02.259
But then there, I was out on the streets in
March for Our Lives in DC for this, and that

00:49:02.259 --> 00:49:04.010
was also important.

00:49:04.010 --> 00:49:08.480
And I love the fact, it was not this one,
but with the prior March for Our Lives, I

00:49:08.480 --> 00:49:16.089
forget who it was, but there was a fantastic,
Black gospel singer who stood up and she sang

00:49:16.089 --> 00:49:25.089
in a gospel style, Bob Dylan's "The Times
They Are A-Changin'" 40 years after it came

00:49:25.089 --> 00:49:26.089
out.

00:49:26.089 --> 00:49:28.380
Gather around, people, the times are changing.

00:49:28.380 --> 00:49:29.839
Don't block up the wall.

00:49:29.839 --> 00:49:34.130
And I listened and it was the very same medicine
that we needed.

00:49:34.130 --> 00:49:37.140
And I felt so glad to be out there with people.

00:49:37.140 --> 00:49:43.279
And I felt so glad that we were doing lobbying,
petitions, and sending money, and trying to

00:49:43.279 --> 00:49:45.690
support the organizations and do what we could.

00:49:45.690 --> 00:49:52.380
And I've also worked with kids coming out
of street gangs in the cities.

00:49:52.380 --> 00:49:53.549
They're veterans too.

00:49:53.549 --> 00:50:01.029
When we've had retreats with Army veterans,
military veterans coming back, combat veterans.

00:50:01.029 --> 00:50:06.809
And some of these young kids, they look at
each other in their eyes and they nod and

00:50:06.809 --> 00:50:12.640
they know that they've all been together in
battle, because some of these kids have seen

00:50:12.640 --> 00:50:16.119
as much shooting as some of these vets have.

00:50:16.119 --> 00:50:19.599
And so we actually need to tend to that.

00:50:19.599 --> 00:50:30.680
And it becomes part of our, I don't know,
our really part of our life purpose in some

00:50:30.680 --> 00:50:31.680
way.

00:50:31.680 --> 00:50:35.760
Yes, to take care of yourself, but yourself
isn't temperate.

00:50:35.760 --> 00:50:38.420
To take care of yourself is everything.

00:50:38.420 --> 00:50:46.319
Tim Ferriss: So, Jack, let me ask you, and
I'm going to say a bit, but the basic question

00:50:46.319 --> 00:50:53.200
is what makes an effective Bodhisattva versus
an ineffective Bodhisattva.

00:50:53.200 --> 00:50:58.710
So the seven habits for the effective Bodhisattva
or something like that is where this is —

00:50:58.710 --> 00:51:06.119
Jack Kornfield: If you can measure it, then
you can change it, right?

00:51:06.119 --> 00:51:07.119
How do we measure it?

00:51:07.119 --> 00:51:08.119
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, right.

00:51:08.119 --> 00:51:13.700
Well, I don't want to over secularize and
pull all the magic out of it, but let me just

00:51:13.700 --> 00:51:14.880
riff for a second.

00:51:14.880 --> 00:51:17.289
You'll see where this is coming from.

00:51:17.289 --> 00:51:21.160
As I mentioned earlier, and I'm going to circle
back to this.

00:51:21.160 --> 00:51:28.920
I've never before noticed so much apathy and
disengagement and anxiety and depression in

00:51:28.920 --> 00:51:29.920
my audience.

00:51:29.920 --> 00:51:34.220
So let's call it 10 to 20 million who I think
are fairly representative across a very broad

00:51:34.220 --> 00:51:40.119
spectrum of, not just American, but global
society, but let's just stick with US society

00:51:40.119 --> 00:51:41.119
at the moment.

00:51:41.119 --> 00:51:44.930
It's every race, every creed, every political
orientation.

00:51:44.930 --> 00:51:52.759
And I think one contributing factor, this
is a theory, is that never before have people

00:51:52.759 --> 00:52:01.470
been exposed to constant global bad news of
every type.

00:52:01.470 --> 00:52:07.420
And so the question of how to be an effective
Bodhisattva stems from something that I've

00:52:07.420 --> 00:52:13.460
been journaling on myself in the last few
weeks, and it came about from reading a revised

00:52:13.460 --> 00:52:16.690
edition of a book called The 80/20 Principle.

00:52:16.690 --> 00:52:23.510
And it talks about, in one section, happiness
islands, and identifying your closest, say,

00:52:23.510 --> 00:52:27.819
10 relationships that bring you peak positive
emotional states.

00:52:27.819 --> 00:52:34.819
And I began to, after I made this list, ask
myself, whenever I got an email asking for

00:52:34.819 --> 00:52:39.289
a coffee date, or a phone call, or fill in
the blank, would I rather spend time with

00:52:39.289 --> 00:52:40.589
this person?

00:52:40.589 --> 00:52:47.029
Or, if I look at my list of top 10 relationships
and ask myself the question, am I spending

00:52:47.029 --> 00:52:49.230
as much time with these 10 people as I would
like?

00:52:49.230 --> 00:52:53.710
If the answer is no, would I be better served,
and would our relationships be better served

00:52:53.710 --> 00:52:56.430
by me focusing on these 10 relationships?

00:52:56.430 --> 00:53:03.400
And I found myself feeling this tension between
trying to serve in my capacity as a writer

00:53:03.400 --> 00:53:12.930
and a host of a podcast, some amorphous larger
group, or would we, on some level, be all

00:53:12.930 --> 00:53:16.579
better served if each of us just said, "Hey,
make a list of your top 10 most important

00:53:16.579 --> 00:53:19.529
relationships and go serve those 10."

00:53:19.529 --> 00:53:21.599
And I'm sure it's not as binder.

00:53:21.599 --> 00:53:23.430
It doesn't need to be one or the other.

00:53:23.430 --> 00:53:27.119
But that's why I asked this question because
there are a lot of well-intentioned people

00:53:27.119 --> 00:53:31.630
who want to do good, but they're so scattered
and their goals are so unclear that they never

00:53:31.630 --> 00:53:33.589
really produce much.

00:53:33.589 --> 00:53:40.690
And that's a very rambling, I think long compound
question, but you see where I'm going now.

00:53:40.690 --> 00:53:46.079
Jack Kornfield: Because it's carried in the
hearts of a lot of the people listening.

00:53:46.079 --> 00:53:51.230
Part of the reason, I think, as you point
out, that people aren't responding maybe as

00:53:51.230 --> 00:53:56.369
objectively as they might, is because they
are overwhelmed, and there is a despair and

00:53:56.369 --> 00:54:02.329
it feels like a helplessness that sweeps over
a lot of people, the problems are too big,

00:54:02.329 --> 00:54:04.289
it's not possible.

00:54:04.289 --> 00:54:12.190
One of my favorite friends, colleagues, mentors
is a woman named Joanna Macy, who's been working

00:54:12.190 --> 00:54:19.579
on nuclear disarmament, climate change issues,
world peace, all kinds of things for decades

00:54:19.579 --> 00:54:21.739
now, she's in her 90s.

00:54:21.739 --> 00:54:30.619
And years ago, she started, I think it was
together with John Seed and other environmentalists,

00:54:30.619 --> 00:54:34.059
a series of practices called the Council for
All Beings.

00:54:34.059 --> 00:54:40.549
And it was part of what she calls despair
and empowerment work.

00:54:40.549 --> 00:54:43.540
And that phrase really carries a lot.

00:54:43.540 --> 00:54:47.791
So people would come, and they'd meditate,
and they'd talk about that issues of climate

00:54:47.791 --> 00:54:49.390
change and so forth.

00:54:49.390 --> 00:54:53.220
And then they would be asked to be silent
for a day or two while they were there in

00:54:53.220 --> 00:54:59.029
some camp, out in nature, and to go out and
find whether in the forest, in the stream

00:54:59.029 --> 00:55:05.729
around, or in their imagination, it could
be the polar bears in the Arctic or whatever.

00:55:05.729 --> 00:55:10.700
Some profound connection that they have with
the natural world of something that they really

00:55:10.700 --> 00:55:13.210
care about.

00:55:13.210 --> 00:55:15.299
And then, because she's playful about it.

00:55:15.299 --> 00:55:17.339
They had all kinds of stuff there.

00:55:17.339 --> 00:55:20.720
She said, "Okay, come back and dress up as
your thing."

00:55:20.720 --> 00:55:25.090
So people came and made a look, that was some
decades ago.

00:55:25.090 --> 00:55:28.019
But in any case, they came back and they made
a circle.

00:55:28.019 --> 00:55:37.720
And in that circle, they were asked to speak
for that part of life that they felt so deeply

00:55:37.720 --> 00:55:38.849
connected to.

00:55:38.849 --> 00:55:41.619
And someone would sit up and say, "I speak
for the salmon.

00:55:41.619 --> 00:55:43.950
I'm a salmon poacher.

00:55:43.950 --> 00:55:46.440
I speak for the salmon.

00:55:46.440 --> 00:55:48.579
Here is what I need.

00:55:48.579 --> 00:55:50.170
And yes, here's my grief.

00:55:50.170 --> 00:55:55.109
And here is what I am empowered to do now."

00:55:55.109 --> 00:55:58.920
Someone else said that "I speak for the redwoods,"
or "I speak for the monarch butterflies,"

00:55:58.920 --> 00:56:06.430
or "I speak for the…" whatever it happened
to be, and it required and invited people

00:56:06.430 --> 00:56:13.190
to be willing to face the very thing you talk
about, the apathy, the despair, and say, "Yes,

00:56:13.190 --> 00:56:15.539
I'll give voice to that."

00:56:15.539 --> 00:56:20.990
And then to bind themselves to something they
care about, like those 10 people in the circle

00:56:20.990 --> 00:56:25.650
that you said that you most care about, maybe
you could expand the circle a little bit further

00:56:25.650 --> 00:56:29.680
and say, "And are there a few other beings
in this world."

00:56:29.680 --> 00:56:35.519
It might be the Ukrainians, or it might be
the polar bears, might be the monarch butterflies,

00:56:35.519 --> 00:56:39.819
or it might be foster children in foster care.

00:56:39.819 --> 00:56:42.009
It's not your task to fix the world.

00:56:42.009 --> 00:56:43.009
That would be hubris.

00:56:43.009 --> 00:56:44.009
:"Okay.

00:56:44.009 --> 00:56:46.589
I'm Jack Kornfield, and I'm the Bodhisattva.

00:56:46.589 --> 00:56:48.749
I'll fix it all."

00:56:48.749 --> 00:56:50.559
Nobody's ever done that, right?

00:56:50.559 --> 00:56:59.579
But it is your task to feel your care and
to extend it to make, to put your oxygen mask

00:56:59.579 --> 00:57:05.549
on, to make something that's safe and sacred,
as best as you can around you, and then extend

00:57:05.549 --> 00:57:08.809
that with your particular gift.

00:57:08.809 --> 00:57:12.390
Your gift is partly through your podcast and
your writing.

00:57:12.390 --> 00:57:20.320
Others, it might be planning a community garden,
or their own amazing garden and giving some

00:57:20.320 --> 00:57:27.210
of the food away, or starting a conscious
business and doing it with the intention to

00:57:27.210 --> 00:57:32.359
make a business that brings well-being to
people who work for it and so forth.

00:57:32.359 --> 00:57:34.150
There are a million ways to do it.

00:57:34.150 --> 00:57:38.769
Nobody can tell you how.

00:57:38.769 --> 00:57:42.751
You have to listen and nobody else — the
other thing is that nobody else can do it

00:57:42.751 --> 00:57:43.751
for you.

00:57:43.751 --> 00:57:46.019
I mean, you are doing it in a way.

00:57:46.019 --> 00:57:48.339
So, you spend plenty of time with those 10
people.

00:57:48.339 --> 00:57:50.619
Hallelujah, I encourage it.

00:57:50.619 --> 00:57:53.479
And I don't believe you're going to stop your
podcast.

00:57:53.479 --> 00:57:54.640
And I don't want you to.

00:57:54.640 --> 00:57:57.539
Tim Ferriss: No, I'm not going to stop my
podcast.

00:57:57.539 --> 00:57:59.109
Jack Kornfield: You got both.

00:57:59.109 --> 00:58:03.069
Actually, in a way you're asking something
that you've, in part, you've already answered.

00:58:03.069 --> 00:58:10.549
So there's a story of a woman who taught in
a middle school or something like that, in

00:58:10.549 --> 00:58:17.539
the Tenderloin in San Francisco and the most
difficult, poorest part of the city, more

00:58:17.539 --> 00:58:21.119
people without homes and so forth.

00:58:21.119 --> 00:58:25.190
And when she got home, she had an apartment
near there.

00:58:25.190 --> 00:58:31.200
If she had the extra bandwidth and if she
wasn't too tired, she would make sandwiches

00:58:31.200 --> 00:58:36.819
and go down and give them to the homeless
people in the neighborhood.

00:58:36.819 --> 00:58:38.911
And little by little people found out about
it.

00:58:38.911 --> 00:58:44.749
And she became a minor celebrity for giving
out the sandwiches.

00:58:44.749 --> 00:58:49.740
And once that was found out, somebody wrote
something about it.

00:58:49.740 --> 00:58:56.839
So she started to receive envelopes of money
"for your good work for the homeless."

00:58:56.839 --> 00:59:03.430
And she answered them all with a one-line
note sent back, she said, "Make your own damn

00:59:03.430 --> 00:59:09.200
sandwiches."

00:59:09.200 --> 00:59:10.200
And that's it.

00:59:10.200 --> 00:59:15.229
It's not that we have to carry it for someone
else, but that each person actually has to

00:59:15.229 --> 00:59:20.499
decide what their gift is.

00:59:20.499 --> 00:59:25.280
And no matter how much despair you have, I
want to ask you on behalf of my grandchildren

00:59:25.280 --> 00:59:34.080
to extend your hand and mend something to
connect with somebody, to do something, not

00:59:34.080 --> 00:59:39.749
just on behalf of them, because actually I
know that it's good for us in our human incarnation

00:59:39.749 --> 00:59:42.329
for our soul, somehow to do that.

00:59:42.329 --> 00:59:45.710
And that's part of the answer to it.

00:59:45.710 --> 00:59:51.160
It's not just despair, but it's empowerment
and realizing that Alice Walker's phrase,

00:59:51.160 --> 00:59:53.789
"We are the ones we've been waiting for."

00:59:53.789 --> 00:59:56.509
It's clear the politicians ain't going to
do it.

00:59:56.509 --> 00:59:58.970
The military, ain't going to do it.

00:59:58.970 --> 00:59:59.970
Who is it?

00:59:59.970 --> 01:00:05.020
Oh, as Miss Piggy would say on Sesame Street,
"Moi?"

01:00:05.020 --> 01:00:09.970
Oh, now we're really in trouble.

01:00:09.970 --> 01:00:14.269
But it also makes a difference.

01:00:14.269 --> 01:00:21.069
And I used this last time when we talked,
because it's an image that I find so compelling

01:00:21.069 --> 01:00:23.810
from Zen Master Thich Nhat Hanh who died recently.

01:00:23.810 --> 01:00:28.999
He said, "When the crowded Vietnamese refugee
boats," and he went to try to help with them

01:00:28.999 --> 01:00:29.999
and things.

01:00:29.999 --> 01:00:36.349
"When the crowded Vietnamese refugee boats
met with storms or pirates, if everyone panicked,

01:00:36.349 --> 01:00:37.369
all would be lost.

01:00:37.369 --> 01:00:44.970
But if even one person on the boat remained
calm and steady, it was enough.

01:00:44.970 --> 01:00:47.769
It showed the way for everyone to survive."

01:00:47.769 --> 01:00:51.609
And then you say, well, who is that one person?

01:00:51.609 --> 01:00:56.670
Because the boat's rocky, the seas are up,
there's anxiety, there's depression.

01:00:56.670 --> 01:01:04.529
But also we are now facing a time of tremendous
moral challenge and opportunity.

01:01:04.529 --> 01:01:11.119
You could either, you succumb in some way
and drown in the depression or the helplessness.

01:01:11.119 --> 01:01:20.099
Course they, you are now being charged in
this time in human history to stand up in

01:01:20.099 --> 01:01:26.039
some way and offer your piece of moral courage
as a voice or an action.

01:01:26.039 --> 01:01:30.140
Your piece of compassion, your Bodhisattva,
which meets just someone who wants to alleviate

01:01:30.140 --> 01:01:32.609
suffering no matter what's happen.

01:01:32.609 --> 01:01:37.270
Being willing to see the suffering from the
despair and say, "That's not the end of the

01:01:37.270 --> 01:01:40.440
story," because it isn't.

01:01:40.440 --> 01:01:45.049
We're survivors for centuries in millennia.

01:01:45.049 --> 01:01:46.069
We know how to do this.

01:01:46.069 --> 01:01:49.509
Tim Ferriss: So I'd like to segue to two things,
Jack.

01:01:49.509 --> 01:01:54.640
The first, just because I keep planting this
flag and I want to make sure I don't break

01:01:54.640 --> 01:01:57.330
any promises to listeners.

01:01:57.330 --> 01:02:05.410
You mentioned Thich Nhat Hanh, whose books
had a huge impact on me, specifically one.

01:02:05.410 --> 01:02:07.630
I don't think it's an anecdote.

01:02:07.630 --> 01:02:11.460
I think it's more of a metaphor, but I remember
it so clearly.

01:02:11.460 --> 01:02:15.990
I can't quite pinpoint why this stuck with
me in the way that it did.

01:02:15.990 --> 01:02:18.130
I'm going to come back to his death, which
I watched.

01:02:18.130 --> 01:02:24.630
I watched his, not his death, but his cremation
and the entire procession on YouTube actually

01:02:24.630 --> 01:02:26.190
live streaming.

01:02:26.190 --> 01:02:33.089
The metaphor, just so I can mention it since
I brought it up, is he talks about doing the

01:02:33.089 --> 01:02:34.089
dishes.

01:02:34.089 --> 01:02:38.440
And if, while you're doing the dishes, you're
thinking of only what you're going to do after

01:02:38.440 --> 01:02:43.700
the dishes that if later you think you're
going to say, enjoy a peach after you finish

01:02:43.700 --> 01:02:47.599
the dishes, that when you are eating the peach,
you're not going to be enjoying the peach.

01:02:47.599 --> 01:02:49.710
You're going to be thinking of whatever comes
after the peach.

01:02:49.710 --> 01:02:55.660
And that's just stuck with me for decades
now, that if you're thinking of something

01:02:55.660 --> 01:02:58.940
else while you're viewing what you're doing
as drudgery and you're looking forward to

01:02:58.940 --> 01:03:02.999
X, chances are when you then think you are
going to enjoy X, you'll be thinking of something

01:03:02.999 --> 01:03:05.440
else if you don't train that present state
awareness.

01:03:05.440 --> 01:03:08.980
So a lot of his teachings have stuck with
me.

01:03:08.980 --> 01:03:11.579
Let's talk about death for a second.

01:03:11.579 --> 01:03:16.499
And what, specifically, the wording that I
have in front of me is what happens as you

01:03:16.499 --> 01:03:17.499
die.

01:03:17.499 --> 01:03:25.969
You have over your many decades of life now
one of the widest ranges of experiences, including

01:03:25.969 --> 01:03:31.700
non-ordinary experiences, of anyone that I've
ever met.

01:03:31.700 --> 01:03:36.210
So please expand on this in any way that you
think makes sense, death and what happens

01:03:36.210 --> 01:03:37.210
as you die.

01:03:37.210 --> 01:03:42.609
Jack Kornfield: I will, but first I want to
pick up Thich Nhat Hanh for a little moment

01:03:42.609 --> 01:03:46.240
of two parts and it will segue into death.

01:03:46.240 --> 01:03:52.430
Christiana Figueres is a Costa Rican diplomat.

01:03:52.430 --> 01:03:55.009
You may have heard of her.

01:03:55.009 --> 01:04:00.890
She said she was the UN representative for
climate, and she helped to organize the Rio

01:04:00.890 --> 01:04:05.320
Climate Summit that happened, I don't know,
a decade ago or so.

01:04:05.320 --> 01:04:10.369
And then it was her job to organize the Paris
Climate Summit that happened, I don't know,

01:04:10.369 --> 01:04:13.490
four years ago or whatever it was.

01:04:13.490 --> 01:04:14.640
And she tells a story.

01:04:14.640 --> 01:04:21.400
She said she had gotten to a place where there
was so much contention among the different

01:04:21.400 --> 01:04:27.380
nations and players about whose fault it was
and who was going to do what, that she became

01:04:27.380 --> 01:04:31.960
really despairing about climate, and about
what we might do.

01:04:31.960 --> 01:04:35.910
Understandably so, and maybe even a bit paralyzed.

01:04:35.910 --> 01:04:41.650
And then somebody said to her, "I think you
need to step out of this despair place for

01:04:41.650 --> 01:04:44.089
a little bit, try something different.

01:04:44.089 --> 01:04:49.659
There's a place down in Southern France run
by this Vietnamese monk named Thich Nhat Hanh…"

01:04:49.659 --> 01:04:51.549
— she'd never heard of him — "...called
Plum Village.

01:04:51.549 --> 01:04:54.519
Why don't you just go there for a couple weeks,
get out of Dodge?"

01:04:54.519 --> 01:04:58.569
And so she did.

01:04:58.569 --> 01:05:03.869
And she said, "I went there," and first of
all, it slowed her down.

01:05:03.869 --> 01:05:05.349
She was not running like we do.

01:05:05.349 --> 01:05:07.799
We watched Thich Nhat Hanh walk.

01:05:07.799 --> 01:05:15.450
And he walks like every step, you were talking
about being present, like every step is a

01:05:15.450 --> 01:05:16.609
miracle, that, "Wow, I'm still alive.

01:05:16.609 --> 01:05:17.609
I can walk.

01:05:17.609 --> 01:05:18.609
The Earth is here.

01:05:18.609 --> 01:05:19.609
Here we are.

01:05:19.609 --> 01:05:20.970
What have I closed my eyes to?

01:05:20.970 --> 01:05:23.059
It's extraordinary."

01:05:23.059 --> 01:05:24.519
Like it was the last day of his life.

01:05:24.519 --> 01:05:26.859
He's just there.

01:05:26.859 --> 01:05:32.410
And then she began to practice with him, to
breathe, to bring some well-being into a body

01:05:32.410 --> 01:05:34.140
that was carrying so much tension and suffering.

01:05:34.140 --> 01:05:38.019
And to let it go to hold it in compassion
to begin to release to.

01:05:38.019 --> 01:05:41.979
He told her to put a little smile on her lips
as she did it.

01:05:41.979 --> 01:05:49.190
And it wasn't like the smile of okay denial,
but it was the smile of the sages of the wise

01:05:49.190 --> 01:05:51.859
ones who've been through everything and say,
"We're still here.

01:05:51.859 --> 01:05:54.140
We know how to do this."

01:05:54.140 --> 01:06:00.930
And then she took the teachings of interdependence
from him of how every breath you take is completely

01:06:00.930 --> 01:06:08.029
breathes with the raccoon in your backyard
and the earthworms.

01:06:08.029 --> 01:06:09.789
It's not a fiction.

01:06:09.789 --> 01:06:10.789
It's the reality.

01:06:10.789 --> 01:06:12.829
We're exchanging air.

01:06:12.829 --> 01:06:13.829
I heard that thing.

01:06:13.829 --> 01:06:18.690
You probably heard it that what's the likelihood
that one breath, that the next breath you

01:06:18.690 --> 01:06:23.079
take will have a molecule from Julius Caesar's
dying breath.

01:06:23.079 --> 01:06:26.529
And the likelihood is 99 percent.

01:06:26.529 --> 01:06:31.420
So I heard that and I come from a family of
scientists, even though I'm a weird one in

01:06:31.420 --> 01:06:32.420
that.

01:06:32.420 --> 01:06:33.799
I thought, "Well, could that be true?"

01:06:33.799 --> 01:06:34.799
So I did the math.

01:06:34.799 --> 01:06:38.680
I said, "Okay, how many molecules?

01:06:38.680 --> 01:06:45.509
Well, in one mold, there's Avogadro's number
and one liter maybe, of that breath.

01:06:45.509 --> 01:06:47.880
There's 10 to the 23rd molecules.

01:06:47.880 --> 01:06:48.880
Okay.

01:06:48.880 --> 01:06:50.210
How many liters of air?

01:06:50.210 --> 01:06:51.210
And I looked it up.

01:06:51.210 --> 01:06:54.410
How many kilometers of atmosphere there is?

01:06:54.410 --> 01:06:55.720
And then how many liters in that.

01:06:55.720 --> 01:06:59.269
It turned out it was 10 to the 22nd.

01:06:59.269 --> 01:07:03.489
I said, "Yeah, 99 percent Julius Caesar's
with you.

01:07:03.489 --> 01:07:05.259
You are breathing everybody."

01:07:05.259 --> 01:07:13.369
Exchanging breath with Gandhi and Mother Theresa,
and Vladimir Putin, and Stalin, and everybody

01:07:13.369 --> 01:07:14.369
else.

01:07:14.369 --> 01:07:15.369
We're in it together.

01:07:15.369 --> 01:07:19.990
She saw it and she said, "Oh, my God, I've
been doing it wrong.

01:07:19.990 --> 01:07:24.950
I've been thinking in terms of victim and
perpetrator, which is how everyone is seeing

01:07:24.950 --> 01:07:25.950
it.

01:07:25.950 --> 01:07:29.420
You're bad and you're being hurt and so forth.

01:07:29.420 --> 01:07:36.769
She went back and she said, "I saw us as a
family, as interconnected, and I wrangled

01:07:36.769 --> 01:07:39.359
196 countries," or whatever it was.

01:07:39.359 --> 01:07:41.339
"And we got the Paris Climate Accord."

01:07:41.339 --> 01:07:46.380
So this is a Thich Nhat Hanh story, but also
a climate story, because it's saying that

01:07:46.380 --> 01:07:51.079
there's another way to see it besides despair
and empowerment.

01:07:51.079 --> 01:07:55.619
So when Thich Nhat Hanh died, he wrote this
thing.

01:07:55.619 --> 01:07:57.150
He taught about it for a long time.

01:07:57.150 --> 01:07:58.660
He said, "I have not died."

01:07:58.660 --> 01:08:01.099
He said, "You just think I've died."

01:08:01.099 --> 01:08:05.819
He said, "But if you look at a cloud, the
cloud turns into snow or rain.

01:08:05.819 --> 01:08:09.220
It falls in the streams and goes in the rivers
and go to the ocean.

01:08:09.220 --> 01:08:12.289
The sun heats the ocean and the cloud forms
again."

01:08:12.289 --> 01:08:13.779
He said, "We are this."

01:08:13.779 --> 01:08:18.029
He said, "I'm not separate from you and the
clouds in the rain.

01:08:18.029 --> 01:08:23.880
We are just the same field of awareness of
consciousness, of being.

01:08:23.880 --> 01:08:28.360
And so I was never born who I really am and
I can never die.

01:08:28.360 --> 01:08:30.040
I'm with you."

01:08:30.040 --> 01:08:34.820
So I was sitting with my father as he was
dying because I'm getting philosophical there,

01:08:34.820 --> 01:08:37.020
but people actually want some — Thich Nhat
Hanh remarkable.

01:08:37.020 --> 01:08:40.000
So I'm sitting with my father.

01:08:40.000 --> 01:08:48.070
Now, my father was, as I said, I think now
slightly mentally ill, paranoid, violent,

01:08:48.070 --> 01:08:52.550
easily triggered and so forth.

01:08:52.550 --> 01:08:55.090
And he was near to dying.

01:08:55.090 --> 01:09:03.340
As I said, he'd been a scientist who'd helped
invent heart lung machines and things like

01:09:03.340 --> 01:09:04.560
that.

01:09:04.560 --> 01:09:05.580
I'll stay with this story.

01:09:05.580 --> 01:09:07.130
There's another little one I might tell.

01:09:07.130 --> 01:09:11.770
But anyway, so I sat with him and he was terrified
of dying.

01:09:11.770 --> 01:09:16.390
He was in the University of Pennsylvania Medical
Center.

01:09:16.390 --> 01:09:23.870
He'd been a student at the University of Pennsylvania,
and he kept looking over at the monitor to

01:09:23.870 --> 01:09:27.550
see if he'd died yet and if anybody would
notice.

01:09:27.550 --> 01:09:32.330
And because he designed some of these monitors,
he was really tuned with — and his fear

01:09:32.330 --> 01:09:33.330
was really great.

01:09:33.330 --> 01:09:35.680
I said, "So Dad, what do you think happens
when you die?"

01:09:35.680 --> 01:09:38.839
And he said, "Nothing, dirt.

01:09:38.839 --> 01:09:41.350
You go back to — I'm a scientist.

01:09:41.350 --> 01:09:42.950
You go back to dirt."

01:09:42.950 --> 01:09:46.910
I said, "Well, if you're a scientist, that's
a presumption.

01:09:46.910 --> 01:09:48.250
I thought scientists did experiments.

01:09:48.250 --> 01:09:50.160
Don't you want to see what happens?"

01:09:50.160 --> 01:09:51.490
He said, "What do you mean, see what happens?"

01:09:51.490 --> 01:09:53.580
I said, "Well, let me tell you my experience.

01:09:53.580 --> 01:10:00.480
See," I said, and I described how when I was
in the monastery, I'm beginning to learn my

01:10:00.480 --> 01:10:03.420
meditation and I got exhausted.

01:10:03.420 --> 01:10:08.710
I did a year in silence sitting in meditation
or walking 20 hours a day.

01:10:08.710 --> 01:10:12.630
And I lay down on the floor one day, completely
exhausted, thinking," I'll get up in 20 minutes

01:10:12.630 --> 01:10:13.630
and keep going.

01:10:13.630 --> 01:10:14.630
I'm going to get enlightened."

01:10:14.630 --> 01:10:17.590
You know how foolish young men are.

01:10:17.590 --> 01:10:23.990
And I got up and I started to do this slow
walking in this cottage I was living in and

01:10:23.990 --> 01:10:28.780
I got to the window, I looked out, I saw my
teacher at a distance in the cottage several

01:10:28.780 --> 01:10:30.730
places over, meeting with somebody.

01:10:30.730 --> 01:10:37.740
I turned around and I walked across the floor
and I saw a body on the floor and I went woo,

01:10:37.740 --> 01:10:41.870
a little rush of fear, and then I realized,
"Oh, God, it's me."

01:10:41.870 --> 01:10:47.170
And I was having my first out-of-body experience
and I went close and I peered at myself, I

01:10:47.170 --> 01:10:49.190
said, "Wow, this is trippy."

01:10:49.190 --> 01:10:52.190
And then — I fell into my body and I'm on
the floor.

01:10:52.190 --> 01:10:55.000
And I stood up and then I learned more how
to do that.

01:10:55.000 --> 01:10:58.100
So I told that to my father.

01:10:58.100 --> 01:10:59.970
I said, "I had past life experiences.

01:10:59.970 --> 01:11:05.200
I remember being a monk in a kitchen in the
Middle Ages in a Chinese monastery and it

01:11:05.200 --> 01:11:06.390
was not fun.

01:11:06.390 --> 01:11:10.580
And then I looked into it and it was how it
was in those days."

01:11:10.580 --> 01:11:14.310
And then I said, "And then I've done past
life regressions with people all around the

01:11:14.310 --> 01:11:19.040
world and people who believe it or don't believe
it, it doesn't matter.

01:11:19.040 --> 01:11:24.490
Half the people have these amazing experiences
and it doesn't matter whether they believe

01:11:24.490 --> 01:11:25.490
it or not.

01:11:25.490 --> 01:11:26.980
It's a complete surprise.

01:11:26.980 --> 01:11:31.280
And I've sat with people and they've died
and they've gotten close and they'll float

01:11:31.280 --> 01:11:32.810
out of their body and then come back."

01:11:32.810 --> 01:11:34.040
And I said, "How's it going?"

01:11:34.040 --> 01:11:37.990
Say, "Well, I started to let go and there
was this whole field of light," which I know

01:11:37.990 --> 01:11:40.990
from meditation and dying and meditation.

01:11:40.990 --> 01:11:43.960
And I was later, there was this sense of love
and bliss.

01:11:43.960 --> 01:11:49.560
And then I did a life review, which happens
in a moment I saw my whole life.

01:11:49.560 --> 01:11:52.170
And what that incarnation was like.

01:11:52.170 --> 01:11:53.770
And I kept telling him these stories.

01:11:53.770 --> 01:11:59.300
I said, "So when you die," I said, "You claim
to be a scientist.

01:11:59.300 --> 01:12:02.410
Why don't you take it as an experiment?

01:12:02.410 --> 01:12:08.740
Because it's likely that you'll find yourself
floating out of your body and you go, 'Whoa,

01:12:08.740 --> 01:12:09.740
what's this?'

01:12:09.740 --> 01:12:12.100
And then there'll be a sense of light and
release.

01:12:12.100 --> 01:12:17.960
And often there's a sense of joy and a sense
of connection to something so much bigger."

01:12:17.960 --> 01:12:19.500
I went on for a little bit.

01:12:19.500 --> 01:12:25.310
I said, "Of course it might happen that you're
right and nothing happens.

01:12:25.310 --> 01:12:30.960
But from my experience inwardly and with so
many people, you're going to start to die

01:12:30.960 --> 01:12:31.980
and you'll float out of your body.

01:12:31.980 --> 01:12:35.190
And there'll be a lot of, it'll be surprises
for you.

01:12:35.190 --> 01:12:40.720
And if that happens, remember, I told you
so."

01:12:40.720 --> 01:12:44.270
Tim Ferriss: How did he respond to this?

01:12:44.270 --> 01:12:47.380
Jack Kornfield: He responded medium.

01:12:47.380 --> 01:12:51.650
It got his curiosity.

01:12:51.650 --> 01:12:54.790
And I felt good, because I at least told him
what I knew.

01:12:54.790 --> 01:12:56.210
We are consciousness.

01:12:56.210 --> 01:12:58.540
You're not your body.

01:12:58.540 --> 01:13:01.670
You have a body, but it's not who you are.

01:13:01.670 --> 01:13:03.240
You're certainly not your thoughts.

01:13:03.240 --> 01:13:04.240
Oh, my God.

01:13:04.240 --> 01:13:07.180
If you were your thoughts, we'd be in trouble.

01:13:07.180 --> 01:13:10.060
They keep changing anyway, one opinion to
another.

01:13:10.060 --> 01:13:15.370
And you're not your emotions, they come like
the tide or the weather.

01:13:15.370 --> 01:13:16.490
So who are you?

01:13:16.490 --> 01:13:21.340
Who you are is the awareness itself that was
born into this body.

01:13:21.340 --> 01:13:24.020
You are consciousness.

01:13:24.020 --> 01:13:29.790
So I got to tell him a little bit of things
that — my side of science, sort of the weird

01:13:29.790 --> 01:13:34.590
science side, and he sort of listened and
I thought, "Well, maybe this'll be useful

01:13:34.590 --> 01:13:35.590
to him."

01:13:35.590 --> 01:13:37.060
Tim Ferriss: All right.

01:13:37.060 --> 01:13:42.690
I have to pick at, not pick, but pick up on
one thing you mentioned in passing because

01:13:42.690 --> 01:13:49.490
a lot of folks are going to harass me, and
it's also piqued my curiosity.

01:13:49.490 --> 01:13:53.730
When you mentioned your first out-of-body
experience, and then I believe you mentioned

01:13:53.730 --> 01:13:56.940
later learning how to do that type of thing.

01:13:56.940 --> 01:14:02.300
How does one learn to do that type of thing
and why would one learn how to do that type

01:14:02.300 --> 01:14:03.300
of thing?

01:14:03.300 --> 01:14:04.300
What is the value?

01:14:04.300 --> 01:14:05.300
Jack Kornfield: All right.

01:14:05.300 --> 01:14:08.370
The first thing is I wondered whether it was
real or not.

01:14:08.370 --> 01:14:12.170
But then when I got back in my body and I
went over to the window and looked out, there

01:14:12.170 --> 01:14:16.233
was my teacher and there was the person visiting
them who I wouldn't have seen while I was

01:14:16.233 --> 01:14:18.450
sleeping.

01:14:18.450 --> 01:14:24.260
More often than not, people's experience of
that kind, and it's actually more common than

01:14:24.260 --> 01:14:31.400
you think, happens in an accident or in some
great distress.

01:14:31.400 --> 01:14:36.620
And literally you could talk to thousands
of people who said, "Oh, I was in this car

01:14:36.620 --> 01:14:40.530
accident, the medics came and there I was
floating above, watching."

01:14:40.530 --> 01:14:44.130
And they can tell you things that happened
that they couldn't possibly have seen, their

01:14:44.130 --> 01:14:45.130
eyes were closed.

01:14:45.130 --> 01:14:46.280
They couldn't see that way.

01:14:46.280 --> 01:14:50.600
So there are thousands of detailed accounts
of that.

01:14:50.600 --> 01:14:56.100
So it's not esoteric and you don't have to
go to the Himalayas or sit in silence for

01:14:56.100 --> 01:14:57.180
a year or something.

01:14:57.180 --> 01:15:03.100
It's actually part of our human nature, whether
it's in accident or some things like that.

01:15:03.100 --> 01:15:08.460
And the simplest thing I can say is that as
I got tremendously concentrated and focused

01:15:08.460 --> 01:15:13.480
in meditation and could move my attention
different places, I could both move it into

01:15:13.480 --> 01:15:17.730
my body and then I learned to move it into
the space of awareness that was not my body.

01:15:17.730 --> 01:15:23.260
And that's enough to say, but it's not that
far away.

01:15:23.260 --> 01:15:26.120
I don't know that it's that valuable per se.

01:15:26.120 --> 01:15:31.670
I'm not counting it, but I learned something,
just like I learned things from taking psychedelics.

01:15:31.670 --> 01:15:32.670
Woo.

01:15:32.670 --> 01:15:33.670
Look at that.

01:15:33.670 --> 01:15:34.670
Everything is dissolving.

01:15:34.670 --> 01:15:36.050
Oh, woo.

01:15:36.050 --> 01:15:37.750
It's not how I thought it was.

01:15:37.750 --> 01:15:41.670
And in fact, and whatever, all kinds of things.

01:15:41.670 --> 01:15:43.980
So I learned something of value from it.

01:15:43.980 --> 01:15:47.940
Tim Ferriss: Well, I may come back to that,
but it sounds like the out-of-body experiences

01:15:47.940 --> 01:15:51.140
wouldn't make the cut into The Seven Habits
of Effective Bodhisattvas?

01:15:51.140 --> 01:15:52.940
Is that what you're saying?

01:15:52.940 --> 01:16:02.280
Jack Kornfield: What makes the cut is knowing
who you are, that you're not limited by the

01:16:02.280 --> 01:16:04.520
identity that you play in the world.

01:16:04.520 --> 01:16:10.400
Because we have all these roles, you're a
podcaster, you're a writer.

01:16:10.400 --> 01:16:16.390
You're the boyfriend or the father or the
grandmother, you're a business person or a

01:16:16.390 --> 01:16:18.670
healer or so forth, all that.

01:16:18.670 --> 01:16:20.060
And all those roles change.

01:16:20.060 --> 01:16:29.100
I mean, one person can be both the father
of somebody and the son of their mother and

01:16:29.100 --> 01:16:30.770
the grandson of their grandmother.

01:16:30.770 --> 01:16:39.600
Well, if you're a policeman and you come home,
there you are, you're on your beat and you've

01:16:39.600 --> 01:16:42.525
got your uniform and your gun and so forth.

01:16:42.525 --> 01:16:50.140
And if that policewoman comes home and tries
to be the policewoman in her family, she will

01:16:50.140 --> 01:16:52.540
have a very unhappy family life.

01:16:52.540 --> 01:16:57.560
She has to not only put her gun down, she
has to put her identity down and become a

01:16:57.560 --> 01:17:04.010
mom or a wife or a daughter to her parent,
or a colleague, friend.

01:17:04.010 --> 01:17:06.910
So we have all these identities.

01:17:06.910 --> 01:17:08.730
They're not who you are.

01:17:08.730 --> 01:17:14.470
They're what your incarnation offers to you
and you get to use them to grow your heart

01:17:14.470 --> 01:17:17.170
and compassion, to understand the world.

01:17:17.170 --> 01:17:23.520
I mean, you can have your own purpose, but
I would invite people to reflect on what would

01:17:23.520 --> 01:17:29.480
be a beautiful purpose for this incarnation,
rather than one where you just suffer.

01:17:29.480 --> 01:17:36.460
And when you start to see that, then it frees
you, then the suffering isn't so much of a

01:17:36.460 --> 01:17:37.460
problem.

01:17:37.460 --> 01:17:44.560
I think of our friend Ram Dass, 20 years in
that wheelchair, half paralyzed from a stroke,

01:17:44.560 --> 01:17:47.710
aphasia, repeated infections, lots of body
pain.

01:17:47.710 --> 01:17:51.230
And by the end of his life, he just became
pure love.

01:17:51.230 --> 01:17:53.370
He said, "I love everything.

01:17:53.370 --> 01:17:54.650
I'm so grateful.

01:17:54.650 --> 01:17:56.190
Like Thich Nhat Hanh for this life.

01:17:56.190 --> 01:17:57.190
I love it.

01:17:57.190 --> 01:17:58.210
I love this wheelchair.

01:17:58.210 --> 01:18:00.480
It's carried me.

01:18:00.480 --> 01:18:01.850
I love those who take care of me."

01:18:01.850 --> 01:18:05.440
He said, "I used to take care of everybody
and start, do all these things.

01:18:05.440 --> 01:18:07.650
Now they have to pick me out of bed and wipe
my butt.

01:18:07.650 --> 01:18:09.310
I didn't like that."

01:18:09.310 --> 01:18:13.520
He said, "I wanted to be the hero who's going
to save everybody.

01:18:13.520 --> 01:18:17.660
And now I have to be a vulnerable human being.

01:18:17.660 --> 01:18:18.670
And I love that too.

01:18:18.670 --> 01:18:21.210
It's taught my heart something even bigger."

01:18:21.210 --> 01:18:24.590
So what do we want to do with this life, basically.

01:18:24.590 --> 01:18:25.600
You each have your measure.

01:18:25.600 --> 01:18:26.800
What do you do with it?

01:18:26.800 --> 01:18:35.460
Tim Ferriss: Well, let me ask you Jack, just
to pick up on finding a beautiful purpose.

01:18:35.460 --> 01:18:41.120
Speaking personally as someone who's generally
oriented towards the world and my environment

01:18:41.120 --> 01:18:47.020
and people around me with vigilance, hyper
vigilance, I think I have focused a lot on

01:18:47.020 --> 01:18:50.830
reducing risk, eliminating pain.

01:18:50.830 --> 01:18:53.000
It's a subtractive approach.

01:18:53.000 --> 01:18:55.460
It's getting rid of things.

01:18:55.460 --> 01:19:02.871
And we've talked about, for instance, contending
with despair, but I have to — well, I don't

01:19:02.871 --> 01:19:09.940
have to, but I imagine that finding or seeking
joy amidst it all is perhaps quite different

01:19:09.940 --> 01:19:13.440
from subtracting the bad things.

01:19:13.440 --> 01:19:17.600
How would you speak to that or suggest people
think about that?

01:19:17.600 --> 01:19:23.390
Jack Kornfield: So first, if you'll forgive
me, I want to read you a poem and it's a full

01:19:23.390 --> 01:19:24.390
page poem.

01:19:24.390 --> 01:19:25.530
So it's not super short.

01:19:25.530 --> 01:19:27.350
And it's one of the great poems.

01:19:27.350 --> 01:19:33.960
I love poetry that I've read in decades from
Jack Gilbert, who is one of our master poets.

01:19:33.960 --> 01:19:35.530
And it's called —
Tim Ferriss: Yes, please.

01:19:35.530 --> 01:19:37.160
Jack Kornfield: — "A Brief for the Defense."

01:19:37.160 --> 01:19:38.560
All right.

01:19:38.560 --> 01:19:40.960
And it speaks to your question.

01:19:40.960 --> 01:19:43.100
Sorrow everywhere.

01:19:43.100 --> 01:19:45.230
Slaughter everywhere.

01:19:45.230 --> 01:19:48.020
If babies
are not starving someplace, they are starving

01:19:48.020 --> 01:19:49.810
somewhere else.

01:19:49.810 --> 01:19:52.690
With flies in their nostrils.

01:19:52.690 --> 01:19:58.560
But we enjoy our lives because that’s what
God wants.

01:19:58.560 --> 01:20:01.960
Otherwise the mornings before summer dawn
would not

01:20:01.960 --> 01:20:03.450
be made so fine.

01:20:03.450 --> 01:20:09.950
The Bengal tiger would not
be fashioned so miraculously well.

01:20:09.950 --> 01:20:13.920
The poor women
at the fountain are laughing together between

01:20:13.920 --> 01:20:19.720
the suffering they have known and the awfulness
in their future, smiling and laughing while

01:20:19.720 --> 01:20:21.590
somebody
in the village is very sick.

01:20:21.590 --> 01:20:22.630
There is laughter
every day in the terrible streets of Calcutta,

01:20:22.630 --> 01:20:23.630
and the women laugh in the cages of Bombay.

01:20:23.630 --> 01:20:32.800
If we deny our happiness, resist our satisfaction,
we lessen the importance of their deprivation.

01:20:32.800 --> 01:20:35.480
We must risk delight.

01:20:35.480 --> 01:20:39.380
We can do without pleasure,
but not delight.

01:20:39.380 --> 01:20:40.440
Not enjoyment.

01:20:40.440 --> 01:20:44.250
We must have
the stubbornness to accept our gladness in

01:20:44.250 --> 01:20:46.670
the ruthless
furnace of this world.

01:20:46.670 --> 01:20:52.360
To make injustice the only
measure of our attention is to praise the

01:20:52.360 --> 01:20:53.360
Devil.

01:20:53.360 --> 01:21:01.610
If the locomotive of the Lord runs us down,
we should give thanks that the end had magnitude.

01:21:01.610 --> 01:21:04.780
We must admit there will be music despite
everything.

01:21:04.780 --> 01:21:05.780
We stand at the prow again of a small ship
anchored late at night in the tiny port

01:21:05.780 --> 01:21:06.780
looking over to the sleeping island: the waterfront
is three shuttered cafés and one naked light

01:21:06.780 --> 01:21:07.780
burning.

01:21:07.780 --> 01:21:08.780
To hear the faint sound of oars in the silence
as a rowboat

01:21:08.780 --> 01:21:09.780
comes slowly out and then goes back is truly
worth

01:21:09.780 --> 01:21:10.780
all the years of sorrow that are to come.

01:21:10.780 --> 01:21:11.780
That's a quite extraordinary poem.

01:21:11.780 --> 01:21:12.780
Tim Ferriss: That really is.

01:21:12.780 --> 01:21:13.780
Who is the author one more time?

01:21:13.780 --> 01:21:14.780
Did you say Jack Gilbert?

01:21:14.780 --> 01:21:16.430
Jack Kornfield: Yes.

01:21:16.430 --> 01:21:22.950
"We must have the stubbornness to accept our
gladness in the ruthless furnace of this world.

01:21:22.950 --> 01:21:27.600
To make injustice the only measure of attention
is to praise the Devil."

01:21:27.600 --> 01:21:29.420
Tim Ferriss: How did you find this poem?

01:21:29.420 --> 01:21:31.200
Or how did it come into your life?

01:21:31.200 --> 01:21:36.190
Jack Kornfield: Oh, I read poetry a lot, and
I don't know where it came from.

01:21:36.190 --> 01:21:40.210
I've had it for some years.

01:21:40.210 --> 01:21:45.250
And I've made friends with some poets and
I've even tried to write poetry.

01:21:45.250 --> 01:21:49.970
Sadly, I'm not very good at it.

01:21:49.970 --> 01:21:51.330
It's like you go to your favorite band.

01:21:51.330 --> 01:21:52.330
All right, I'm young.

01:21:52.330 --> 01:21:54.210
I'm going to go hear The Dead.

01:21:54.210 --> 01:21:56.700
I wish I could play with them.

01:21:56.700 --> 01:22:02.790
I can't, but I'm a great fan.

01:22:02.790 --> 01:22:05.160
And we're lucky because we have the great
poets of the world.

01:22:05.160 --> 01:22:10.980
We have Hafez and Rumi and we have the Chinese
poets, we have Li Po and we have all these

01:22:10.980 --> 01:22:12.180
magnificent poets.

01:22:12.180 --> 01:22:17.810
My good friend Luis Rodriguez, who was the
Poet Laureate of Los Angeles couple of years

01:22:17.810 --> 01:22:23.060
ago and is a Latino activist who's worked
with gangs and worked in prison because he

01:22:23.060 --> 01:22:24.060
was in a gang.

01:22:24.060 --> 01:22:29.770
In fact, his first major book which wasn't
poetry was called [Always Running: La Vida

01:22:29.770 --> 01:22:36.290
Loca: Gang Days in L.A.], which he says proudly
is the most stolen book in high school libraries

01:22:36.290 --> 01:22:39.170
across America.

01:22:39.170 --> 01:22:42.290
And his platform as a poet, he's running for
governor.

01:22:42.290 --> 01:22:48.250
He wants to be, if he can, come in second,
so he can run against Newsom.

01:22:48.250 --> 01:22:49.440
He knows he won't win.

01:22:49.440 --> 01:22:52.140
He said, "But I want to stand up for the homeless.

01:22:52.140 --> 01:22:55.490
I want to stand up for the farm workers.

01:22:55.490 --> 01:23:00.140
What kind of culture it is that these people
all work to feed us and they don't have enough

01:23:00.140 --> 01:23:02.240
food stamps to feed their children?

01:23:02.240 --> 01:23:09.710
I want to stand up for climate and for public
transportation and alternatives."

01:23:09.710 --> 01:23:11.030
He's got all this, it's a beautiful platform.

01:23:11.030 --> 01:23:17.330
Tim Ferriss: Jack, let me ask you if I could,
just before we move to your friend, "A Brief

01:23:17.330 --> 01:23:19.920
for the Defense," why did you read that?

01:23:19.920 --> 01:23:21.440
And what do you make of the title also?

01:23:21.440 --> 01:23:26.260
Jack Kornfield: "A Brief for the Defense,"
because it's in defense of joy.

01:23:26.260 --> 01:23:28.530
And I'll go back to it with you, I'm happy
to.

01:23:28.530 --> 01:23:35.940
André Gide, the great French philosopher
said, "When you understand correctly, you

01:23:35.940 --> 01:23:39.610
must embrace joy as a moral obligation."

01:23:39.610 --> 01:23:42.790
And we'll stay with that for a moment.

01:23:42.790 --> 01:23:46.270
Joy, for there's a book and now a movie that
came out, The Book of Joy.

01:23:46.270 --> 01:23:48.320
You may have seen it.

01:23:48.320 --> 01:23:57.020
That's a dialogue between Archbishop Desmond
Tutu, who recently died, and the Dalai Lama

01:23:57.020 --> 01:23:58.390
about joy.

01:23:58.390 --> 01:24:04.870
And part of what's in that book, it's mostly
in — a record of their dialogue.

01:24:04.870 --> 01:24:09.710
They each acknowledged immense suffering that
they've been through and the people around

01:24:09.710 --> 01:24:10.710
them.

01:24:10.710 --> 01:24:15.980
For Tutu, the incredible suffering and under
Apartheid and all the conflicts and the people

01:24:15.980 --> 01:24:22.640
who were killed and died, and many who he
knew and thrown in prison and what tore apart

01:24:22.640 --> 01:24:25.200
the nation, really.

01:24:25.200 --> 01:24:27.440
And he was right in the center of it.

01:24:27.440 --> 01:24:33.330
And the Dalai Lama of course, fleeing from
Tibet, having the Chinese army burn the sacred

01:24:33.330 --> 01:24:39.280
texts and the monasteries and imprison monks
and nuns and take over the culture.

01:24:39.280 --> 01:24:42.120
And as he said, "They've taken so much from
me.

01:24:42.120 --> 01:24:45.560
Why should I let them take my happiness?"

01:24:45.560 --> 01:24:49.080
And they talk to each other and they're both
very funny.

01:24:49.080 --> 01:24:52.340
They both have great senses of humor.

01:24:52.340 --> 01:25:02.080
And they say, for them, like Gide's moral
obligation, the world is too magnificent and

01:25:02.080 --> 01:25:07.270
mysterious and too beautiful to live on the
channel of despair.

01:25:07.270 --> 01:25:10.190
It's just not.

01:25:10.190 --> 01:25:16.420
And so in the Buddhist tradition, we have
these practices where you cultivate joy, where

01:25:16.420 --> 01:25:21.320
you look through the eyes to see first the
secret beauty and everybody going by.

01:25:21.320 --> 01:25:29.650
And one of the parts of that practice of joy,
and you could try it, Tim, is when you look

01:25:29.650 --> 01:25:37.020
at somebody, try to imagine their happiest
day, their happiest moment as a young child,

01:25:37.020 --> 01:25:44.120
because even if they had a bad childhood,
some do, there were days when they were tickling

01:25:44.120 --> 01:25:50.840
and wrestling and rolling around and laughing,
I think there's an enviable spirit in children

01:25:50.840 --> 01:25:56.520
— I've seen it in refugee camps — that
just can't be stopped, an innocence.

01:25:56.520 --> 01:26:03.870
And so you look at that person and you see
not that adult with all their cares and traumas

01:26:03.870 --> 01:26:10.710
and whatever, their fear and anger, but you
realize that child of the spirit is in them.

01:26:10.710 --> 01:26:13.500
It actually still is.

01:26:13.500 --> 01:26:21.360
And then you start to remember your most beautiful,
happy days as a child, happy moment somehow.

01:26:21.360 --> 01:26:23.080
And then you invite and invoke it.

01:26:23.080 --> 01:26:24.750
May I live with that spirit?

01:26:24.750 --> 01:26:28.410
May I see it in others?

01:26:28.410 --> 01:26:40.330
So Tim, I want to pick up with the thread
of joy coming out of that poem and Gide's

01:26:40.330 --> 01:26:46.600
sense of joy as a moral obligation because
people can also often think about meditation

01:26:46.600 --> 01:26:49.370
as something like a grim duty.

01:26:49.370 --> 01:26:53.540
"All right, I've got to go to therapy and
I've got to work out and I've got to diet

01:26:53.540 --> 01:26:54.920
and I should meditate."

01:26:54.920 --> 01:27:01.300
As you know, as if it's some self-help thing,
and especially since you asked, when your

01:27:01.300 --> 01:27:04.730
nervous system, as all of ours, is in disarray
right now.

01:27:04.730 --> 01:27:07.670
Somewhat in vigilant and wary.

01:27:07.670 --> 01:27:16.250
Meditation is not supposed to be a grim duty,
but rather more an act of adventure and understanding

01:27:16.250 --> 01:27:20.940
of opening the mind and heart and bringing
a sense of well-being.

01:27:20.940 --> 01:27:29.010
Mahatma Gandhi said, "What use is freedom
if it doesn't also include the freedom to

01:27:29.010 --> 01:27:32.090
make mistakes?"

01:27:32.090 --> 01:27:35.630
And my teacher Ajahn Chah, we would ask him
different questions about things.

01:27:35.630 --> 01:27:39.780
And a lot of the times he would say, "It's
uncertain, isn't it?"

01:27:39.780 --> 01:27:40.990
"Tell me about enlightenment."

01:27:40.990 --> 01:27:41.990
He'd smile.

01:27:41.990 --> 01:27:43.230
He'd say, "It's uncertain, isn't it?"

01:27:43.230 --> 01:27:45.070
Or "Tell me what happens when you die."

01:27:45.070 --> 01:27:46.590
"It's uncertain, isn't it?"

01:27:46.590 --> 01:27:50.880
I mean, he had teachings, but he loved the
wisdom of uncertainty.

01:27:50.880 --> 01:27:55.760
He said, "Why don't you be just as you talked
about?"

01:27:55.760 --> 01:28:01.910
Tim, when you're washing the dishes, as Ajahn
described, to feel the soapy water in your

01:28:01.910 --> 01:28:07.520
hands and enjoy washing the dishes or being
with another person.

01:28:07.520 --> 01:28:14.600
I remember reading about these Russian astronauts
who got in trouble and were not sure they

01:28:14.600 --> 01:28:19.430
would even make it back from their orbit above
the Earth where they'd been for a while.

01:28:19.430 --> 01:28:24.360
But finally they did and their capsule came
down and opened and they said they touched

01:28:24.360 --> 01:28:29.930
the Earth with their feet and it was the most
glorious moment just to be in the dusty plain

01:28:29.930 --> 01:28:37.130
of Kazakhstan and touch the ground and the
plants that people might have called weeds.

01:28:37.130 --> 01:28:41.370
To see the eyes of people who were walking
around, meeting them, or to be able to go

01:28:41.370 --> 01:28:45.730
home and sit at a table with people felt absolutely
miraculous.

01:28:45.730 --> 01:28:53.770
And so there's something about meditation
when we quiet the mind and yes, we can bring

01:28:53.770 --> 01:29:00.340
compassion and tend to all the difficulties
that we carry, but it's an invitation to get

01:29:00.340 --> 01:29:04.190
bigger, to feel the sense of mystery.

01:29:04.190 --> 01:29:10.670
As the poet says, "I'm moving at tremendous
speed toward a star in the Milky Way.

01:29:10.670 --> 01:29:17.030
My heart's a little fast, otherwise everything
is fine."

01:29:17.030 --> 01:29:26.420
And for you, and those who listen, to remember
that spirit of adventure and joy that was

01:29:26.420 --> 01:29:30.280
there as a child, the child of the spirit.

01:29:30.280 --> 01:29:33.270
So if you would Tim, close your eyes for a
moment.

01:29:33.270 --> 01:29:39.990
Let's just meditate together and other people
can join us and take a couple of long breaths

01:29:39.990 --> 01:29:48.670
and let yourself settle.

01:29:48.670 --> 01:29:53.090
And notice that there are different channels.

01:29:53.090 --> 01:29:59.210
Consciousness has these different dimensions
and you can tune into the channel of despair

01:29:59.210 --> 01:30:06.620
or the one of outrage, but you can also tune
into the channel of the Dalai Lama and two,

01:30:06.620 --> 01:30:12.080
two, and so forth of adventure and caring
and joy.

01:30:12.080 --> 01:30:24.070
And as you sit quietly, let yourself remember
one of the best moments of your childhood,

01:30:24.070 --> 01:30:32.240
even vaguely, one of the best days of joy,
laughing, playing, enjoying, tickling the

01:30:32.240 --> 01:30:38.670
child of the spirit, and keeping your eyes
closed, feel it.

01:30:38.670 --> 01:30:43.820
And let me know if you can remember something
like that.

01:30:43.820 --> 01:30:45.050
Tim Ferriss: I can.

01:30:45.050 --> 01:30:47.310
Jack Kornfield: What's it feel like in your
body?

01:30:47.310 --> 01:30:48.310
Stay with it.

01:30:48.310 --> 01:30:49.840
Tim Ferriss: It feels light.

01:30:49.840 --> 01:30:53.010
Unencumbered, feels expansive.

01:30:53.010 --> 01:31:01.460
Jack Kornfield: So stay with —
Tim Ferriss: Exploratory.

01:31:01.460 --> 01:31:04.630
Jack Kornfield: Beautiful.

01:31:04.630 --> 01:31:13.090
So stay with this, the quality of light and
unencumbered, expansive, exploratory, joyful.

01:31:13.090 --> 01:31:24.040
And let it fill your body, and let it fill
your cells and DNA to remind you that this

01:31:24.040 --> 01:31:31.990
child of the spirit, this inviable joy that
was born into you is still really available.

01:31:31.990 --> 01:31:35.000
It can't be taken from you no matter what.

01:31:35.000 --> 01:31:55.090
So just let it open and filling your whole
being in an easy, joyful way.

01:31:55.090 --> 01:32:07.730
And now let yourself imagine that some being
who carries the spirit of joy and wisdom,

01:32:07.730 --> 01:32:10.300
amidst everything in human life.

01:32:10.300 --> 01:32:23.170
Some amazing being comes to join you know
as a guide, as a friend, as inspiration and

01:32:23.170 --> 01:32:30.230
let yourself be surprised who wants to come
with carrier of joy, and wisdom and meet it

01:32:30.230 --> 01:32:39.530
all, and see who wants to arrive or appear.

01:32:39.530 --> 01:32:46.440
And allowing this, keeping your eyes closed.

01:32:46.440 --> 01:32:52.700
Notice, let me know who it, if anyone comes.

01:32:52.700 --> 01:32:56.010
Tim Ferriss: Well, it's actually a friend
of mine is younger than I am.

01:32:56.010 --> 01:32:57.860
Who I think does a pretty good job.

01:32:57.860 --> 01:32:58.990
Jack Kornfield: Good, good, good.

01:32:58.990 --> 01:33:07.520
Tim Ferriss: With levity and adventure, while
also being really effective in the world.

01:33:07.520 --> 01:33:12.390
Jack Kornfield: So both adventurous and joyful,
adventurous, and effective.

01:33:12.390 --> 01:33:16.730
And so this friend, guy, male, female?

01:33:16.730 --> 01:33:19.530
Tim Ferriss: Male, in this case.

01:33:19.530 --> 01:33:24.720
Jack Kornfield: This fellow comes and he's
now with you and you see his smile and maybe

01:33:24.720 --> 01:33:29.570
he puts his arm around your shoulders just
like your brother, "We're in this together.

01:33:29.570 --> 01:33:32.000
Come on, let's have a good time.

01:33:32.000 --> 01:33:34.900
Let's make an adventure where we make a difference.

01:33:34.900 --> 01:33:36.970
And we do it with real joy."

01:33:36.970 --> 01:33:44.670
And let yourself feel that connection so that
you're together in this.

01:33:44.670 --> 01:33:47.820
We need that from one another.

01:33:47.820 --> 01:33:56.680
And then as you feel this, he reaches into
his pocket and he has a gift for you.

01:33:56.680 --> 01:34:03.740
That's a clear symbol of what you need to
step out of that small sense of self that

01:34:03.740 --> 01:34:06.170
you've talked about.

01:34:06.170 --> 01:34:11.840
That's vigilant, hypervigilant and learn to
try to protect yourself.

01:34:11.840 --> 01:34:14.840
But he knows you're much bigger than that.

01:34:14.840 --> 01:34:20.570
And he has the perfect symbol, the gift that
will help you to be who you really are.

01:34:20.570 --> 01:34:28.180
This joyful, vast, adventurous spirit, and
see what he gives to you.

01:34:28.180 --> 01:34:30.510
He places it in your hands.

01:34:30.510 --> 01:34:33.570
If it's not clear, you can lift it up to the
light.

01:34:33.570 --> 01:34:34.600
So you can see it.

01:34:34.600 --> 01:34:35.670
Just what you need.

01:34:35.670 --> 01:34:40.930
What do you get from him?

01:34:40.930 --> 01:34:46.790
Tim Ferriss: Would you like my answer?

01:34:46.790 --> 01:34:47.960
So I don't know why.

01:34:47.960 --> 01:34:56.150
Well, maybe there is no why, but it looks
like a very small maybe inch and a quarter

01:34:56.150 --> 01:35:04.800
inch and a half diameter, almost like pendant,
but it's a silver circle with some type of

01:35:04.800 --> 01:35:10.650
circular bluish green stone in the center,
which is round.

01:35:10.650 --> 01:35:12.470
Jack Kornfield: All right.

01:35:12.470 --> 01:35:13.691
So hold it in your hand.

01:35:13.691 --> 01:35:19.570
And then you can ask him, what does this symbolize?

01:35:19.570 --> 01:35:21.340
He'll tell you.

01:35:21.340 --> 01:35:24.720
You can know.

01:35:24.720 --> 01:35:32.740
Your intuition, your understanding will come.

01:35:32.740 --> 01:35:40.990
What does he tell you it symbolizes?

01:35:40.990 --> 01:35:42.040
Tim Ferriss: Unending connection.

01:35:42.040 --> 01:35:46.110
The circularity of it, just the illusion of
separateness.

01:35:46.110 --> 01:35:49.460
Jack Kornfield: So hold it in your hand.

01:35:49.460 --> 01:35:54.040
And he's reminding you that part of the joy
and the adventure you can have is that you're

01:35:54.040 --> 01:35:55.500
connected with it all.

01:35:55.500 --> 01:35:59.130
You're always connected that circularity.

01:35:59.130 --> 01:36:00.240
And there's a joy in that.

01:36:00.240 --> 01:36:04.940
A spirit you can't fall out of that circle,
because it is you.

01:36:04.940 --> 01:36:06.250
You are life.

01:36:06.250 --> 01:36:13.860
And then he whispers in your ear, a few words
of support for living with joy.

01:36:13.860 --> 01:36:22.180
What does he say?

01:36:22.180 --> 01:36:23.830
Tim Ferriss: The first thing that came to
mind was "Take it easy!

01:36:23.830 --> 01:36:24.830
Take it easy!"

01:36:24.830 --> 01:36:25.830
Jack Kornfield: Okay.

01:36:25.830 --> 01:36:27.360
Listen to that.I've heard the song, myself.

01:36:27.360 --> 01:36:33.840
And you're laughing as you say it and feel
that laughter fill your body because he's

01:36:33.840 --> 01:36:39.540
really reminding you who you are, that you
can do this, that this is actually not just

01:36:39.540 --> 01:36:43.770
a channel, but this is underneath all the
rest that we try to do.

01:36:43.770 --> 01:36:49.910
Here we are, we try to be grown up responsible,
protect ourselves, take care of everything.

01:36:49.910 --> 01:36:50.910
Take it easy.

01:36:50.910 --> 01:36:54.130
You can do what you do as he does.

01:36:54.130 --> 01:37:08.740
Adventure and success for this joyful spirit,
and pause and just feel it.

01:37:08.740 --> 01:37:10.480
How is this?

01:37:10.480 --> 01:37:14.890
Tim Ferriss: It feels good.

01:37:14.890 --> 01:37:16.490
Feels lighter.

01:37:16.490 --> 01:37:19.940
There's a certain unwinding sensation to it.

01:37:19.940 --> 01:37:20.980
Jack Kornfield: Beautiful.

01:37:20.980 --> 01:37:29.560
So you're kind of demonstrating in your own
experience now for other people who are listening,

01:37:29.560 --> 01:37:30.560
watching.

01:37:30.560 --> 01:37:34.530
You're really demonstrating what meditation
can do.

01:37:34.530 --> 01:37:37.940
It can open you from what's called the body
of fear.

01:37:37.940 --> 01:37:45.530
The small sense of self to expansiveness and
to a sense of connection and well-being.

01:37:45.530 --> 01:37:52.030
And as you found that kind of adventure and
joy, now, the beautiful thing is that even

01:37:52.030 --> 01:37:58.810
if you're traveling and you happen to go to
Costa Rica or Indonesia, or wherever you go,

01:37:58.810 --> 01:38:01.650
that he's not far away.

01:38:01.650 --> 01:38:09.320
You can close your eyes and he'll join you
in spirit, wherever you are.

01:38:09.320 --> 01:38:14.050
Because as in that circle, he's part of who
you are.

01:38:14.050 --> 01:38:18.860
And it's always available in five minutes.

01:38:18.860 --> 01:38:22.260
And this is one of the gifts of meditation.

01:38:22.260 --> 01:38:28.570
And it's not like Pollyanna because there's
a lot of difficult things we have to carry.

01:38:28.570 --> 01:38:33.200
And Mara keeps coming back with progression
and doubt and so forth.

01:38:33.200 --> 01:38:35.370
And you can say, "Yes, I see you, Mara.

01:38:35.370 --> 01:38:36.680
I know you're here."

01:38:36.680 --> 01:38:42.031
And there's the Buddha seating seated there
saying "Yes, Mara, I see you.

01:38:42.031 --> 01:38:47.920
Oh, you're Mara, but that's not who I am."

01:38:47.920 --> 01:38:53.040
Oh, it's a pleasure.

01:38:53.040 --> 01:38:58.470
Pleasure to sense you in this way, because
I know it's true for you.

01:38:58.470 --> 01:39:01.900
So then you wanted to ask a question about
loving-kindness.

01:39:01.900 --> 01:39:04.340
Tim Ferriss: I did.

01:39:04.340 --> 01:39:14.480
I was inclined to ask about loving-kindness
meditation because as you and I have discussed,

01:39:14.480 --> 01:39:17.139
think quite a few years ago, so it's been
a little while.

01:39:17.139 --> 01:39:21.840
It seems to be sometimes an effective on ramp
for westerners who have trouble with other

01:39:21.840 --> 01:39:23.900
forms of meditation.

01:39:23.900 --> 01:39:30.530
I don't know if that's a fair statement to
make, but I do recall for the first time doing

01:39:30.530 --> 01:39:33.410
loving-kindness meditation myself, very short
meditations.

01:39:33.410 --> 01:39:39.420
I mean really five minutes is what we're talking
about, but doing it every night before bed,

01:39:39.420 --> 01:39:42.100
when I was working on Tools of Titans, this
book of mine.

01:39:42.100 --> 01:39:51.320
And it was incredible to look at the before
and after effect on my default state of being,

01:39:51.320 --> 01:39:57.730
after five or six or seven days of just doing
five minutes consistently of loving-kindness

01:39:57.730 --> 01:40:01.590
meditation, it was quite shocking to be honest.

01:40:01.590 --> 01:40:05.990
And for that reason, I thought I would ask
if there's any loving-kindness meditation

01:40:05.990 --> 01:40:08.960
you might recommend for people who would like
to give it a shot?

01:40:08.960 --> 01:40:12.231
Jack Kornfield: Sure, well course I'm glad
you had that experience.

01:40:12.231 --> 01:40:13.900
And it is common for people.

01:40:13.900 --> 01:40:16.210
And sometimes just sit in meditation.

01:40:16.210 --> 01:40:21.150
You sit, you close your eyes, and even if
you're just trying to breathe and feel your

01:40:21.150 --> 01:40:22.650
breath and calm.

01:40:22.650 --> 01:40:29.900
The mind is racing or the emotions of worry
or anxiety or other things kind of take you

01:40:29.900 --> 01:40:30.900
over.

01:40:30.900 --> 01:40:34.940
And it doesn't feel like it's — at the first
it's not easy.

01:40:34.940 --> 01:40:37.440
And it even doesn't feel like it's working.

01:40:37.440 --> 01:40:41.670
It does work but in the beginning they can
feel that way.

01:40:41.670 --> 01:40:44.610
The wisdom of what you're saying is quite
right.

01:40:44.610 --> 01:40:51.460
That to actually deliberately turn the attention
toward loving-kindness meditation or something

01:40:51.460 --> 01:40:57.960
like it, the joy practice that we just started
to do, and it doesn't take long in five minutes.

01:40:57.960 --> 01:41:01.740
You just did five minutes and it changed your
state.

01:41:01.740 --> 01:41:08.610
The other practice that's also good is a walking
meditation, especially if you're upset.

01:41:08.610 --> 01:41:13.520
Sometimes it's actually not that good to try
to sit, but we move naturally pace anyway,

01:41:13.520 --> 01:41:19.780
but this is more deliberately like Thich Nhat
Hanh walking with a kind of deliberate care

01:41:19.780 --> 01:41:24.920
and attention and maybe bringing in a little
loving-kindness meditation.

01:41:24.920 --> 01:41:31.110
Take each step for yourself or the people
that open your heartless naturally, that you

01:41:31.110 --> 01:41:32.880
care about.

01:41:32.880 --> 01:41:36.810
So if people go onto my website, jackkornfield.com.

01:41:36.810 --> 01:41:40.460
There are a whole series of loving-kindness
meditation.

01:41:40.460 --> 01:41:45.830
If you click on meditations, primarily audio,
there's a couple with videos, but primarily

01:41:45.830 --> 01:41:54.210
audio and it helps to have a voice guiding
you through it at least for the first while.

01:41:54.210 --> 01:41:59.460
And then as you did, you can take it on and
find your own language and do five minutes

01:41:59.460 --> 01:42:05.720
a day or 10 minutes, and it will change your
consciousness really very quickly.

01:42:05.720 --> 01:42:07.040
And then I love doing it.

01:42:07.040 --> 01:42:13.630
I can be on the airplane flying somewhere
and I'm reading my book or lost in whatever

01:42:13.630 --> 01:42:15.980
I'm doing and it feels very isolating.

01:42:15.980 --> 01:42:22.540
And then I'll take three minutes and I'll
look up and I'll look down the aisle.

01:42:22.540 --> 01:42:24.290
There's an old grandmother.

01:42:24.290 --> 01:42:25.350
And I wish her well.

01:42:25.350 --> 01:42:30.750
May she be safe and happy and loving-kindness
surround her with loving-kindness.

01:42:30.750 --> 01:42:34.240
And then there's that pierced teenager over
there.

01:42:34.240 --> 01:42:39.450
May he be well and surrounded with loving-kindness
and then there's that old man over there.

01:42:39.450 --> 01:42:45.890
And I just do two or three minutes of it kind
of almost like offering a blessing of wishing

01:42:45.890 --> 01:42:47.000
them well.

01:42:47.000 --> 01:42:54.020
And then when we land and we get off, I could
almost like wave goodbye to those folks because

01:42:54.020 --> 01:42:56.240
they're like, "Oh, yeah, I got connected with
you.

01:42:56.240 --> 01:42:58.630
You don't know it, but we are connected."

01:42:58.630 --> 01:43:01.560
It changes everything and it happens.

01:43:01.560 --> 01:43:02.990
We're walking down the street.

01:43:02.990 --> 01:43:04.720
It can happen in a moment.

01:43:04.720 --> 01:43:11.770
So some of the other things just to recommend,
I have a book entitled The Art of Forgiveness,

01:43:11.770 --> 01:43:18.470
Lovingkindness, and Peace, which has both
stories in it and written on instructions

01:43:18.470 --> 01:43:22.780
for joy practice and loving-kindness and compassion
practice.

01:43:22.780 --> 01:43:25.550
Along with those things on my website.

01:43:25.550 --> 01:43:32.600
And I do have this, what people are finding
to be a kind of amazing teacher training program

01:43:32.600 --> 01:43:38.040
for people who are interested in learning
it for yourselves and passing on these practices.

01:43:38.040 --> 01:43:42.670
You mentioned that MMTCP mindfulness meditation
certification program.

01:43:42.670 --> 01:43:47.720
Also, you can find on my website and it's
a two-year program.

01:43:47.720 --> 01:43:51.490
There's ways to start to learn meditation
if you haven't.

01:43:51.490 --> 01:43:56.240
And we've now done it for 5,000 people in
70 countries.

01:43:56.240 --> 01:44:03.630
And I get stories like this woman, who's in
Lahore, Pakistan, who said, "After I've gone

01:44:03.630 --> 01:44:08.130
through this, now I'm teaching this in a girl
school in Pakistan.

01:44:08.130 --> 01:44:12.840
And it's so helpful for the girls to have
the compassion and loving-kindness training

01:44:12.840 --> 01:44:14.490
and joy practices."

01:44:14.490 --> 01:44:18.330
Or I get a note from a guy who says, "Yeah,
I'm in Kenya.

01:44:18.330 --> 01:44:23.030
I work in a clinic and yes, we need attend
to people medically, but they also really

01:44:23.030 --> 01:44:25.240
need medicine for the heart."

01:44:25.240 --> 01:44:28.610
And I teach people and there's so much gratitude.

01:44:28.610 --> 01:44:32.800
So for anybody interested, it's a wonderful
training and that's also available.

01:44:32.800 --> 01:44:40.720
And yeah, I feel really blessed Tim, because
somehow looking for adventure like you, and

01:44:40.720 --> 01:44:47.880
as we do when we're young, I decided to go
into this Buddhist monastery and it was tough,

01:44:47.880 --> 01:44:52.920
which of course we want as young people to
be initiated, to try something difficult,

01:44:52.920 --> 01:44:54.840
to see what we can learn.

01:44:54.840 --> 01:44:59.880
And as my teacher said, "Where have you learned
the most when it was easier when it was difficult?"

01:44:59.880 --> 01:45:02.940
So somehow humanity has to learn this right
now.

01:45:02.940 --> 01:45:12.300
We're in a place where the deepest sense of
what the world needs of our courage, our steadiness,

01:45:12.300 --> 01:45:18.550
our being that one person on the boat that
Ajahn Chah talked about to be calm and steady

01:45:18.550 --> 01:45:20.060
in the face of these storms.

01:45:20.060 --> 01:45:28.870
And it shows a way for us to reconnect and
not just to survive, but actually to be able

01:45:28.870 --> 01:45:34.370
to instill a sense of graciousness or love
well-being or love.

01:45:34.370 --> 01:45:41.530
And I think that one of the other little practices
that I find a wonderful reflection in the

01:45:41.530 --> 01:45:51.560
morning when I meditate or get quiet is the
question, what would love have me do today?

01:45:51.560 --> 01:45:54.180
What would love have me do today?

01:45:54.180 --> 01:45:58.760
And then the heart starts to answer.

01:45:58.760 --> 01:46:02.760
And some of it's tough that we have to attend
to this and do that.

01:46:02.760 --> 01:46:05.510
But what would love have me do today?

01:46:05.510 --> 01:46:12.190
And what we're talking about is listening
and trusting our own intuition and trusting

01:46:12.190 --> 01:46:15.870
our own heart to guide us.

01:46:15.870 --> 01:46:22.240
I have a little passage to read, which are
instructions from a thousand years ago or

01:46:22.240 --> 01:46:30.220
a long time ago by this Tibetan master to
meditation master teacher named Atisha.

01:46:30.220 --> 01:46:32.540
And here they are, it's really what we've
been talking about.

01:46:32.540 --> 01:46:34.780
And here's how we talked about it.

01:46:34.780 --> 01:46:37.780
He said advice, Atisha's advice.

01:46:37.780 --> 01:46:42.810
He said, "Consider all experience to be dreams.

01:46:42.810 --> 01:46:50.010
We take it all so seriously, but there's this
vastness of the galaxies and millions of people

01:46:50.010 --> 01:46:54.990
being born and dying and peace coming and
war coming and joy and sorrow and gain and

01:46:54.990 --> 01:46:58.460
loss and consider it all to be dreams.

01:46:58.460 --> 01:47:02.680
Be grateful to everyone.

01:47:02.680 --> 01:47:04.880
Thank you to everyone.

01:47:04.880 --> 01:47:08.310
Even the ones that make difficulty.

01:47:08.310 --> 01:47:13.450
Don't be swayed by outer circumstances.

01:47:13.450 --> 01:47:16.430
Don't brood over the faults of others."

01:47:16.430 --> 01:47:20.560
I love this one, because it's so easy to judge
the others.

01:47:20.560 --> 01:47:26.840
"Explore the timeless nature of consciousness.

01:47:26.840 --> 01:47:30.170
Let suffering teach you compassion.

01:47:30.170 --> 01:47:35.760
At all times, rely on a joyful mind.

01:47:35.760 --> 01:47:39.970
And the last is don't expect a standing ovation."

01:47:39.970 --> 01:47:44.900
I like that one.

01:47:44.900 --> 01:47:46.350
It's not a performance.

01:47:46.350 --> 01:47:50.740
It's really living your own life, becoming
the Buddha that you are.

01:47:50.740 --> 01:47:58.210
The wise one, the wise woman or the wise man,
the sage, really embodying that you just found

01:47:58.210 --> 01:48:05.120
in yourself with your friend with that kind
of joy that's both able to accomplish things

01:48:05.120 --> 01:48:10.490
but able to do it in a way that plants seeds
of well-being and adventure and caring.

01:48:10.490 --> 01:48:12.960
Tim Ferriss: Thank you, Jack.

01:48:12.960 --> 01:48:19.030
I want to bring up also one quote that you
shared with me long ago.

01:48:19.030 --> 01:48:20.060
This is quite a few years ago.

01:48:20.060 --> 01:48:21.990
I think it was in our first conversation on
the podcast.

01:48:21.990 --> 01:48:28.860
And this is, I believe a quote, you can correct
me if I'm wrong, from Ryōkan Taigu, who is

01:48:28.860 --> 01:48:32.590
a quiet and unconventional Soto Zen Buddhist
monk — I'm reading from Wikipedia — who

01:48:32.590 --> 01:48:34.570
lived much of his life as a hermit.

01:48:34.570 --> 01:48:36.110
Also well known for poetry and calligraphy.

01:48:36.110 --> 01:48:41.230
But the quote that you shared was, and let
me just make sure I get this right.

01:48:41.230 --> 01:48:47.040
This is Ryōkan referring to himself, "Last
year, a foolish monk, this year.

01:48:47.040 --> 01:48:48.190
No change."

01:48:48.190 --> 01:48:54.480
So when you're judging yourself harshly, maybe
just also remembering that this business of

01:48:54.480 --> 01:48:59.610
being human, isn't always easy, even for people
who focus on cultivating a lot of these faculties.

01:48:59.610 --> 01:49:01.340
Jack Kornfield: Perfect.

01:49:01.340 --> 01:49:04.220
"Last year, a foolish monk.

01:49:04.220 --> 01:49:09.110
This year, no change."

01:49:09.110 --> 01:49:12.090
Laughter in his spirit as a poet can be.

01:49:12.090 --> 01:49:13.610
Thank you, Tim.

01:49:13.610 --> 01:49:14.610
Always.

01:49:14.610 --> 01:49:16.150
Tim Ferriss: Yes, absolutely.

01:49:16.150 --> 01:49:19.740
And I will include links for everyone in the
show notes to everything we referred to.

01:49:19.740 --> 01:49:20.740
So jackkornfield.com.

01:49:20.740 --> 01:49:26.840
@JackKornfield on Twitter, and on Instagram
Jack_kornfield, and for those interested in

01:49:26.840 --> 01:49:35.700
the loving-kindness meditations on your meditations
